{"type":"message","id":"069ca66d","parentId":"d8f6c6c4","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:09:56.318Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll mark task 1 as in progress, then dispatch the exploration. Two Explore agents (the 9B gateway serializes requests, so two at a time should be fine — but the docs warn against parallelizing the 9B; two concurrent Explore agents would be a serialized request on the gateway — slow but should work). The safer approach is: one Explore at a time. Actually, let me think about the total budget:\n\n9 work units. If I do everything myself in the parent, context becomes a concern (I have a 170k window, and a compaction policy is also set up). Work units 1 and 3 are the core. Let me structure this:\n\n1. Explore agent #1: Survey the current state of WU1 (the county coverage resolver and its consumers) — read-only, file:line handoff.\n2. Explore agent #2: Survey the current state of WU3 (claim handling in copilot-actions.ts, the execute route, execution records).\n3. After that, implement WU1 and WU3 myself in the parent (high reasoning).\n4. Meanwhile, Explore agent #3: Survey WU2/WU4/WU5/WU6/WU7 (mechanical units) — one agent can cover all of these (they're all small, well-defined files).\n5. After that, delegate the mechanical implementation to the ImplementationWorker 9B (one per unit), and I'll verify.\n6. WU8: write the missing tests — this is a larger writing task; either delegate the mechanical test writing to the worker, or do it myself.\n7. WU9: run the full verification suite myself in the parent (long-running commands via bash), and honestly update the closeout document.\n\nWait — the plan says: \"County reconciliation and Copilot transaction work should be kept on the high-reasoning lane.\" I'm the 27B parent; \"high reasoning\" maps to my thinking level. I can use request_deeper_reasoning once for complex work. Alright.\n\nLet me launch the first Explore. Since the gateway is serialized, to avoid