{"type":"message","id":"e9787fa9","parentId":"379bd7cf","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:10:29.066Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"File sizes are manageable. Key files for WU1:\n- `src/lib/county-records/capabilities.ts` (428 lines) — probably `resolveCountyCoverage`\n- `src/lib/county-records/provider.ts` (119)\n- `src/lib/county-records/config.ts` (154)\n- Consumer side: `src/app/api/properties/`, `src/app/api/screening/`, `src/app/api/actions/` (county-related?), `src/lib/agent-worker.ts`, `src/lib/recorded-documents/`\n\nWU3: `src/lib/copilot-actions.ts` (940 lines) + `src/app/api/copilot/`.\n\nFor the units I'm implementing myself (WU1, WU3), I'll need to read these in the parent. The total comes to a substantial amount (about 2500 lines of reading). My context is 170k; the docs I've already read were large (final report ~10k words, etc.). I have room but need to be careful: prioritize the highest-signal files, and use module_report/read_symbol to get the structure before the full read.\n\nPlan:\n1. Launch Explore agent #1 (9B, read-only) to investigate WU1 + WU3 current state with file:line references — it handles broad reads (consumer routes, worker, seeding), so it protects my context.\n2. In the meantime, dispatch Explore #2? No — it's sequential execution. Launch #1 first.\n\nActually, to save round-trips, I'll write a very precise brief for Explore #1 covering both WU1 and WU3. Then while that's running… no, I can't do other subagent work in the parent during the wait. The Explore is running in the background; I can do other work in the meantime (reading specific files myself). Let me launch the Explore in the background and start reading the most critical files myself in parallel: capabilities.ts and copilot-actions.ts.\n\nWait, \"don't parallelize live 9B calls. The gateway serializes requests\" — two Explore agents would be serialized by the gateway anyway, and that just doubles the latency. Also, while an Explore is