{"type":"message","id":"07ba2183","parentId":"5ebc06e4","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:16:05.550Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-a08987535628f185","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server';\n\nimport { NO_STORE, requireRecordedProperty } from './shared';\nimport { getLatestRecordedSearch } from '@/lib/firestore/recorded-documents';\n\nexport const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';\n\n/**\n * GET /api/properties/[id]/recorded-documents\n *\n * The property's latest recorded-document search run — or `not_started`\n * when it has never been searched. This is a read of the SERVER-OWNED\n * search state (the worker writes it); the client never writes it.\n */\nexport async function GET(\n  request: NextRequest,\n  { params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }\n) {\n  try {\n    const auth = await requireRecordedProperty(request, params);\n    if (auth instanceof NextResponse) return auth;\n\n    const search = await getLatestRecordedSearch(\n      auth.user.id,\n      auth.property.id\n    );\n    if (!search) {\n      return NextResponse.json(\n        {\n          status: 'not_started',\n          searchId: null,\n          propertyId: auth.property.id,\n          manualNextSteps: [\n            'Search the county recorder indices by parcel number and record the results manually.',\n          ],\n        },\n        { headers: NO_STORE }\n      );\n    }\n    return NextResponse.json(search, { headers: NO_STORE });\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Error in GET .../recorded-documents:', error);\n    return NextResponse.json(\n      { error: 'Internal server error' },\n      { status: 500, headers: NO_STORE }\n    );\n  }\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788196565550}}
{"type":"message","id":"73bf3a54","parentId":"07ba2183","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:16:54.033Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a grasp of the overall picture of the recorded documents read path:\n\n- `GET /recorded-documents` (root) — returns the latest search (or `not_started`) — no coverage guard.\n- `GET /recorded-documents/searches/[searchId]` (polling) — returns the search — no coverage guard.\n\nWU1 requires applying the canonical coverage guard to both: \"Unavailable properties must not expose stale county-specific results as current authoritative data.\"\n\nDesign decision on the shape of the guard: the search run itself is a historical artifact (it has mode, index, and documents). Rather than hiding it (data loss) or deleting it, the honest behavior is: when the property's coverage is no longer `live`, the read returns the search envelope with the current coverage and a typed \"no-longer-authoritative\" marker, so the UI can render the result as stale history rather than current truth. The plan says \"must not expose stale county-specific results as current authoritative data\" — meaning the response must be distinguishable.\n\nConcretely:\n- The route computes `coverage = resolveCountyCoverage(property.county, countyRecord?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId)` (the same input as the search POST gate — consistency!).\n- If `coverage.status === 'live'`: return the search as it currently is (no shape change → zero UI risk).\n- If not live: return the search with added fields `coverage` (a client-safe projection) and `authoritative: false` + a message. Or, better: return an envelope `{ search: <existing shape>, coverage: <projection>, staleReason: 'coverage_not_live' }`. But that changes the shape of the response, and the UI's `isSearchResponse` check would fail (`'status' in value`).\n\nHmm, the UI checks `isSearchResponse(payload)` = an object with a `status` in SEARCH_STATUSES. If I wrap it in an envelope, the UI's loadState breaks (it would set \"Recorded documents could not be loaded.\"). So there are two options:\n  a) Keep the search document as the top level and add optional fields to it (`coverage`, `authoritative`). `isSearchResponse` still passes (status is present). The UI can then render a banner when `authoritative === false`. This is backward-compatible and minimal.\n  b) An envelope + UI changes to unwrap.\n\nOption (a) is cleaner and aligns with \"the UI already receives the search.\" Add optional fields to the `RecordedSearch` type: `coverage?: { status, county, message }` — hmm, but RecordedSearch is a domain type (persisted). Better not to pollute the persisted domain type with a derived presentation field. Define `RecordedSearchWithCoverage = RecordedSearch & { coverage: CoverageProjection; authoritative: boolean }` in types, and have the route return that. `isSearchResponse` passes because it's a structural type check on `status`.\n\nNow — what exactly does \"unavailable property\" mean for the recorded documents? A property where the resolved coverage is not live (planned / not_covered / unknown / or a new review state). If the coverage degrades after a search run has completed, that run's documents were fetched from the county recorder — that county's source. If the property is now claimed to be in a different county (contradiction!), those results are not authoritative for the property. So the guard is: the canonical coverage computed the same way as the search gate.\n\nImportant detail — the search gate uses `property.county` + `gateRecord.snapshot.provenance.sourceId`. For the read guard, use the exact same inputs (property + county record). The county record for the read guard is `getCountyRecord(uid, propertyId)`.\n\nAnother WU1 element: **idempotent county reconciliation persistence**. The current backfill: `backfillWatchlistCounty` — read; if it exists, return false; if county is already set, return false; `ref.set({county}, {merge:true})`. Problems: (1) not atomic (a concurrent explicit PATCH could slip in between the read and the set — a race where the backfill overwrites the user's explicit county: read no county → user PATCHes Cuyahoga → backfill writes the Cuyahoga from the source… if it's the same county it's harmless, but if the source is a different county, the backfill overwrites the user's assertion — this is exactly the \"an explicit assertion is never overwritten\" violation under a race); (2) the worker wraps it in try/catch and swallows failures.\n\nWU1 plan: \"Add an idempotent persistence/reconciliation operation for countyless legacy records. Do not swallow persistence failures or infer from state/address.\"\n\nDesign: a new function in firestore/properties.ts (or county-records) — `reconcileWatchlistCounty(uid, propertyId, county, expectedProvenance)`. Run it in a transaction:\n  - Read the property document inside the transaction.\n  - If it doesn't exist → return a typed result `{status: 'property_gone'}` (nothing to reconcile; not an error).\n  - If county is already set → compare with the target county (base slug); if identical → `{status: 'already_reconciled'}`; if different → don't overwrite the explicit assertion → `{status: 'explicit_conflict'}` (surfaced as a review signal, no write).\n  - If county is unset → write {county, countyReconciledAt, countyReconciledFrom: <source id>} → `{status: 'reconciled'}`.\n  - On persistence error → throw (don't swallow) — the worker handles it: what does the worker do on failure? The plan says \"don't swallow persistence failures\" — so the worker must surface it: a county_confirm completion where reconciliation fails → the task fails (retryable) rather than succeeding silently. Hmm, note: currently backfill is at the worker's confirm path — I need to check where in the worker backfillCountyBestEffort is called from. Let me look at the worker's confirm flow (runCountyConfirm / runCountyRefresh) — I've seen part of it. If reconciliation fails, the county snapshot is already saved (domain write complete) — failing the task would block the confirmed state, which is worse. Let me look at the actual call sites.\n\nActually — a deeper question: what does \"reconciliation\" mean beyond the worker backfill? Plan: \"add an idempotent persistence/reconciliation operation for countyless legacy records\" — an operation, meaning an explicit callable (route? worker step? both?). Given \"all of property detail, /api/coverage, screening input, county mutation routes, worker selection, recorded document routes, and task creation must consume the same result\" — the read path resolves coverage from provenance without needing a write; reconciliation writes the county onto the property so future reads are consistent even without provenance (e.g., if the county record is deleted, or if the property list /api/coverage doesn't read the county record).\n\nWhere to hook the write: the county_confirm path (worker + the confirm route) is the natural place — the parcel is confirmed from a source → the property's county is proven. Let me check the current confirm route (confirm/route.ts) — does it call backfill? And the worker's runCountyConfirm. Let me grep for callers of backfillWatchlistCounty.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86c820a900fbac78","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -rn \"backfillWatchlistCounty\\|backfillCountyBestEffort\" src/ | grep -v __tests__; echo \"===\"; grep -n \"runCountyConfirm\\|runCountyRefresh\" src/lib/agent-worker.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9969e75fbc3e7d36","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":80,"offset":55,"path":"/Users/evanhuang/brickspect/dashboard/.worktrees/records-agent-launch/src/lib/firestore/county-records.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":118608,"output":1738,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1585,"totalTokens":120346,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cache