{"type":"message","id":"034a8012","parentId":"578f5675","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:18:24.311Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now, regarding the WU1 design decisions, there's a point that genuinely requires deep consideration: the semantics of fail-closed for contradictions. Let me think about what I know so far and whether a deeper reasoning invocation is warranted:\n\nThe central design questions:\n1. When the explicit county conflicts with the provenance of the confirmed snapshot, should `resolveCountyCoverage` return a `live` result (current behavior: the explicit one takes precedence), or should it fail-closed? The plan says: \"an explicit county that conflicts with the snapshot's provenance must fail-closed and surface a typed review state.\" So, yes — a contradiction ⇒ not-live, a typed review state. This changes the shape of CountyCoverage (a new discriminant is needed). All consumers (routes return notCoveredPayload, workers throw WorkerBlockedError, the UI renders the banner) must handle it.\n\n2. A typed review state: options are — (a) add a 5th status 'review' to CountyCoverage, or (b) keep the 4 statuses and add a blocking `contradiction` that consumers must check. Adding a 5th status is cleaner for fail-closed: every `if (coverage.status !== 'live')` automatically fails-closed, and the UI can render a different message for review. However, this also changes notCoveredPayload (which currently maps non-live → reason planned/not_covered/unknown) — 'review' needs its own reason/message. Also, the registry-status field for county identity in review state: which county to display? The explicit one (what the user asserted) — and the note about the mismatch.\n\nI think the design is sufficiently constrained by the plan's wording (\"fail-closed and surface a typed review state\"). I don't think this is \"genuinely difficult\" in the sense that I can't solve it carefully myself — I have the full context. However, the plan explicitly states: \"keep county reconciliation and Copilot transaction work on the high-reasoning lane.\" I am the parent, and I can request a deeper reasoning invocation (xhigh) — it's available once. The instruction is: \"use request_deeper_reasoning only after investigating the task and relevant code; provide a concise rationale showing that additional reasoning substantially improves accuracy. Do not use it for routine edits…\" This qualifies: the WU1 contradiction design ripples through about 6 route surfaces + worker + UI + a large test suite; a single xhigh pass could reduce the risk of subtle semantic errors (e.g., does the contradiction fail-closed apply when the explicit county equals the provenance? No — only when the base differs; what about fixture vs. live source IDs — both map to the same county, no contradiction; a legacy property that is county-less but has a snapshot of provenance X with the explicit county being X — no contradiction, and live).\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me reconsider: is xhigh worth it? I can already reason through this design carefully myself. The deeper reasoning invocation returns \"one xhigh-reasoning response\" — i.e., my next response is generated at xhigh. This is about my own inference quality on the next turn. Since I'm about to write the design of a multi-file change that touches the core fail-closed semantics, an xhigh next turn is a reasonable use. Let me request it with concrete rationale.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6498edad2f59bf0","name":"request_deeper_reasoning","arguments":{"reason":"The next edit is the WU1 core: turning the recorded-but-permissive county contradiction (explicit county vs confirmed-snapshot provenance) into a fail-closed typed review state across ~6 route surfaces + worker + UI + a 428-line capabilities module and its test suite. Subtle semantics (base-slug comparison, fixture/live source-id equivalence, interaction with the explicit-wins backfill, notCoveredPayload's reason union, and zero-write guarantees) make an xhigh pass materially reduce the risk of a wrong fail-closed boundary."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":124588,"output":846,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":703,"totalTokens":125434,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788196677093,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a2d1f71d3bcb8a62","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
