message, safe to render verbatim. */\n  message: string;\n}\n\nexport type StoredCountyRecordStatus =\n  | 'confirmation_required'\n  | 'no_match'\n  | 'confirmed'\n  | 'source_error'\n  | 'unsupported_county';\n\n/** The persisted record: one document per watched property. */\nexport interface StoredCountyRecord {\n  schemaVersion: typeof COUNTY_RECORDS_SCHEMA_VERSION;\n  /**\n   * Denormalized owner (the document always lives under `users/{uid}`;\n   * server consumers must never re-derive identity from the path alone).\n   * Backfilled from the path on the first CAS write of a legacy doc.\n   */\n  ownerUid: string;\n  /**\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788196904612}}
{"type":"message","id":"e313c33b","parentId":"913469c9","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:23:57.814Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have everything I need for the WU1 design. Time to nail down the design and implement. Let me carefully write out the design:\n\n## WU1 Design — Canonical County Reconciliation\n\n### 1. Fail-closed contradiction (capabilities.ts)\n\nCurrent state: `resolveCountyCoverage(county, confirmedSourceId)` — when an explicit county conflicts with the provenance-derived county (differing base slugs), it records `contradiction` and returns the explicit county's registry status (live/planned/not_covered).\n\nNew behavior: when a contradiction exists, the result must fail closed with a typed review state.\n\nDesign decision — a 5th status: add `status: 'review'` to `CountyCoverage`:\n\n```ts\n| {\n    status: 'review';\n    county: CountyIdentity;          // the explicitly declared county (what the user is seeing)\n    eta: null;\n    source: null;\n    contradiction: { explicit: CountyIdentity; provenance: CountyIdentity };\n  }\n```\n\nSemantics:\n- `review` means: an explicit county and a provenance-derived county from a confirmed snapshot conflict, and county work is blocked until a human resolves it. It is **not** live.\n- `notCoveredPayload` gets `reason: 'review'` (extending the union `'planned' | 'not_covered' | 'unknown' | 'review'`), with a clear message.\n- All `coverage.status !== 'live'` gates automatically fail closed for review (422).\n- Worker's `requireLiveCounty`: review → WorkerBlockedError('county_review'? or 'unsupported_county'?) — hmm, the worker's error code: 'unsupported_county' is used for non-live. For a review contradiction, the plan says \"surface a typed review state.\" Let's use a distinct worker error code `county_review_required`... wait, let me check the values of WorkerBlockedError and which codes the task's status can take — need to be careful: the worker's blocked codes probably get persisted on the task. Let me check the agent-tasks type for blocked codes. I'll verify before deciding.\n\nWhich county to display in the review state: the explicit one (what the user declared) — and mention both counties in the contradiction note.\n\nEdge cases:\n- Explicit = null (no county) → no contradiction possible (contradiction requires explicit != null). Provenance alone → live (for a covered county). No change.\n- Explicit Cuyahoga + provenance Cuyahoga (same base, even with a different state token) → no contradiction (base comparison).\n- Explicit Cuyahoga + provenance null (no confirmed snapshot) → no contradiction.\n- Explicit Summit (planned) + provenance Cuyahoga (confirmed live) → contradiction → REVIEW. This is the important case: even if provenance is live, the explicit one is planned, but a contradiction fails closed.\n- Explicit Cuyahoga + provenance Summit (if such a snapshot existed — impossible since Summit has no source, but hypothetically) → review.\n\n### 2. Provenance-aware consumers\n\n- **Property detail GET** (`properties/[id]/route.ts:69`): read `getCountyRecord(user.id, id)` and pass `record?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId ?? null`. The response's coverage will henceforth include the backfill → consistent with the worker.\n- **Property detail PATCH** (`:171`): after the county update, the new county may conflict with the existing confirmed snapshot → recompute coverage with the same provenance input so the response reflects the new review state. (PATCH's response already returns `coverage`.)\n- **/api/coverage**: currently explicitly by design \"lightweight projection.\" The plan says all surfaces must agree. To make /api/coverage agree, it would have to read the county record for every watchlist property (N additional reads — a batchable query: the countyRecords subcollection per property... that's N subcollections; Firestore has no cheap cross-subcollection query. Hmm.)\n\nWait — let me think about this more carefully. Is a per-property county-record read in /api/coverage acceptable? The watchlist can be large. N individual reads of `properties/{id}/countyRecords/current` — that's N round trips. Could use a batch (Firestore batch get — 500 limit). Reasonable for a small N.\n\nAlternative design: at reconcile time, persist the county onto the property (the new transactional reconcile), so after a legacy property has been reconciled once, all surfaces agree without a per-property record read: an explicit county is present → /api/coverage's explicit-only resolution returns the same result. The only divergence is for legacy properties that have a confirmed snapshot but have not yet been reconciled.\n\nPlan text: \"have property detail, /api/coverage, screening inputs, county mutation routes, worker selection, recorded-document routes, and task creation consume the same result.\" — \"consume the same result\" = the same canonical resolver with the same inputs. For /api/coverage, the cleanest way to honor both the performance note and consistency: use the canonical resolver with provenance — read the county record per property via a batched read (bounded, documented). Since /api/coverage already does listWatchlistItems (1 read), adding a batched county-record read for items **without** an explicit county only is bounded and cheap (only legacy candidates need the record; explicit ones short-circuit — explicit county + no snapshot = no contradiction; explicit county + snapshot of the same county = no contradiction; only when a county is absent, or... wait, contradiction requires an explicit county AND a differing snapshot).\n\nHmm wait: contradiction can also happen when an explicit county exists AND there's a confirmed snapshot of a different county. So a batched read for **all** properties with an explicit county that could conflict is needed... but that's all of them. Except: a snapshot only exists on records with status confirmed / refresh-failed / no_match-with-snapshot? Actually a snapshot is only set after confirm (and refresh preserves the last known-good). For a property with an explicit county, a snapshot of a different county can only exist if: (a) the property's county was changed after confirm (user PATCHed a different county) — rare; (b) legacy data.\n\nTo be safe and simple: for /api/coverage, batch-read the county record for every watchlist property (batch get, 1 round trip per ≤500). For a demo-sized watchlist this is fine; document the cost. Actually — even simpler and fully correct: batch-read for every property; resolve each with `resolveCountyCoverage(explicit, record?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId)`. Group by county identity (for review status, use the explicit county identity + the review flag).\n\nHmm, but hold on — does this change the /api/coverage grouping semantics for review rows? A review property groups under its explicit county code and is displayed with status 'review'. Let me keep the row shape (status is now a 5-value union including 'review').\n\nLet me check how the UI consumes /api/coverage (the dashboard's coverage card + the properties list pill) and how the deep-dive banner uses coverage — to design the client-side additions. I'll look at those consumers during implementation.\n\n### 3. Guard on recorded-document reads\n\n- `GET /recorded-documents` (root) and `GET /recorded-documents/searches/[searchId]`: compute coverage with exactly the same inputs as the search POST gate: `resolveCountyCoverage(property.county ?? null, countyRecord?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId ?? null)`.\n- If coverage is live → return the search as-is (shape unchanged).\n- If coverage is not live → return the search (or not_started) with extra fields: `authoritative: false`, `coverage: <client-safe projection including status + message>`. If live → `authoritative: true` (or omit).\n- Additive fields → the UI's `isSearchResponse` keeps passing. UI update: when `authoritative === false`, show a notice banner (\"county coverage has changed — these results are no longer authoritative\"). This is part of WU1's UI surface.\n\nHmm wait — there's a subtlety for the **not_started** root response: when a property is not live, the root read currently returns a `not_started` payload (with manualNextSteps). If the property has never been searched, there's nothing stale to expose — but the manual step \"search the county recorder indices...\" is only appropriate for a covered county. Add the coverage field there too (consistent envelope).\n\nType: extend `RecordedSearchResponse` — add an optional `coverage?: CoverageNote` to the RecordedSearch type? The domain type is also used by worker/firestore. Cleaner: a new response type `RecordedSearchReadResponse = (RecordedSearch | RecordedSearchNotFound) & { authoritative: boolean; coverage: CoverageNote }`. Hmm — but then `isSearchResponse` in the component still passes (structural). The component will need to render the note. Let me define in types/recorded-documents.ts:\n\n```ts\nexport interface CoverageNote {\n  status: 'live' | 'planned' | 'not_covered' | 'unknown' | 'review';\n  county: { code: string; name: string } | null;\n  message: string | null;\n}\n```\n\nAnd have the route produce it via a shared helper in capabilities.ts — `coverageNote(coverage)` — used in the two read routes (and reusable in the WU2 provenance work).\n\n### 4. Transactional idempotent reconcile\n\nNew function in `src/lib/firestore/properties.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport type CountyReconcileOutcome =\n  | { status: 'reconciled'; item: WatchlistItem }\n  | { status: 'already_reconciled'; item: WatchlistItem }\n  | { status: 'explicit_conflict'; item: WatchlistItem; conflicting: CountyIdentity }\n  | { status: 'property_gone' };\n\nexport async function reconcileWatchlistCounty(\n  uid: string, propertyId: string, county: CountyIdentity\n): Promise<CountyReconcileOutcome>\n```\n\n- runTransaction: get the property doc.\n  - Doesn't exist → 'property_gone' (no write).\n  - County present (parseCountyIdentity non-null):\n    - Same base slug (compare with normalizeCountyCode + countyBase) → 'already_reconciled' (no write).\n    - Different → 'explicit_conflict' (no write — an explicit assertion is never overwritten).\n  - County absent → set { county, countyReconciledFrom: <sourceId>, countyReconciledAt: now } → 'reconciled'.\n- Never infer: only called with a provenance-derived county.\n- No swallowing: the function throws on a genuine persistence failure (the transaction error propagates) — the caller decides.\n\nCallers (worker auto-confirm, confirm, refresh; and the synchronous confirm route):\n- Replace `backfillCountyBestEffort` with a `reconcileCounty` helper in the worker: call `reconcileWatchlistCounty`; on 'explicit_conflict' → don't fail the task? Hmm. If a user has explicitly set Summit on a property whose parcel is confirmed from Cuyahoga — that's the review state. The confirm action itself (the snapshot write) is legitimate (the record's county was Cuyahoga by provenance at enqueue time... wait, actually if the explicit county is Summit (planned, not live), `requireLiveCounty` would have blocked the task before the confirm (coverage = review → non-live → WorkerBlockedError). So within the worker, an explicit_conflict at backfill time is only reachable when the county was PATCHed to a different county between enqueue and run — the coverage check is before the provider call... let me re-check the order: requireLiveCounty runs before the provider call (at the top of runCountyConfirm). If a PATCH happens in between (the window), the backfill sees a different explicit county → explicit_conflict → must not overwrite; the snapshot write has already been committed (confirmCountyParcel is after requireLiveCounty). So the state: the property has explicit Summit + a confirmed Cuyahoga snapshot → the world is in review; the task succeeded (the confirm applied); the reconcile is recorded as a conflict. The task should be marked successful (the domain action happened) — the conflict is surfaced by the read-side review state (coverage = review on all surfaces). But per the plan \"don't swallow persistence failures\" — an explicit_conflict is not a persistence failure; it's a typed outcome. A real write failure (transaction error) → for the worker: what to do? The confirm is already durable; the reconcile write failed. Options: mark the task failed (retryable — retry re-attempts the confirm → stale revision → blocked, not good) or log + return success with a note.\n\nHmm, let me think about what is actually safe and honest here. The purpose of reconcile: consistency of county identity across surfaces. If the write fails, the property remains county-less, but the confirmed snapshot is present → all provenance-aware surfaces (property detail, worker, search route) still resolve it as live via provenance; only /api/coverage (if explicit-only) shows unknown. With my /api/coverage change (provenance-aware), the surfaces all agree even without the write. So a reconcile failure degrades only the convenience of explicit identity — it doesn't break the fail-closed invariant. But the plan explicitly says: \"don't swallow persistence failures.\" So: the worker must surface it — log at error level (not warn), and... the plan's zero-write/zero-swallow concern is about not silently pretending success. Implementation idea: on reconcile persistence failure, the worker records a structured error log with all identifiers (uid, property, county) and returns task success with a `reconcileFailed` note on the success payload? The task-success type is `{ eventType, record }` — extending it ripples.\n\nA simpler, defensible interpretation: \"don't swallow persistence failures\" = the reconcile operation itself must not catch/ignore errors (the current backfillCountyBestEffort does `try { ... } catch { console.warn }` — that's the swallow). The new `reconcileWatchlistCounty` propagates errors; the worker catches it in one place and treats it as a **retryable sub-failure that does not roll back the confirm**: mark the task success (the confirm is durable), but emit a typed error log + an outbox-free side note? Emitting an extra event might break the projection.\n\nLet me look at how the worker's failure path works — WorkerBlockedError vs retryable. Actually — the cleanest is: have the worker call reconcile and on a genuine error: log at `error` level (distinct from the previous warn) and continue with the success path, but include a `reconcileDegraded: true` marker on... hmm, no, don't fabricate a new persisted state.\n\nRe-reading the plan's WU1 acceptance: \"all surfaces return one consistent county feature. No route picks Cuyahoga from state alone, and legacy reconcile is durable and idempotent.\" — \"durable\" = the write is transactional (atomic with the read check), and if it fails, the system notices (error log) and retries (a later confirm/refresh re-attempts; idempotent). I'll do this: the worker's reconcile error → `console.error` with full context, and the confirm is still marked successful (the domain mutation is complete) — plus the next confirm/refresh (reconcile is idempotent) re-attempts automatically. This is \"documented recovery\" rather than swallowed. And I'll document this boundary in the module header (crash boundary: snapshot write and county reconcile are two transactions; a failure in between leaves a property with a confirmed snapshot and no explicit county — all surfaces still agree via provenance; reconcile is retried on subsequent county work).\n\nAdditionally, add reconcile to the synchronous confirm route (the route-level confirm currently doesn't backfill — I found that gap). In the route: after enqueue (or after the worker confirms... wait — the synchronous confirm route just **enqueues** a county_confirm task; the worker does the confirm. So the route doesn't own the confirm; the worker is the single owner of the backfill. I was wrong above: \"the confirm route doesn't call backfill — only the worker does (3 sites)\" — right, all three of the worker's sites cover auto-confirm, confirm, refresh. The route just enqueues. So no route change needed for the write path.\n\n### 5. Worker error codes\n\nCheck what WorkerBlockedError codes exist and how they get persisted (the blocked code on the task). If I add a new code 'county_review_required' — need to check that the task type allows it. Let me check the blocked codes in types/agent-tasks.ts. I'll do this during implementation.\n\n### Tests (WU1)\n\nPer the plan:\n1. Unit matrix: explicit Cuyahoga, planned, unsupported, unknown, legacy confirmed Cuyahoga, explicit/provenance contradiction → all in the capabilities.test.ts extension (add review status cases: explicit Cuyahoga + provenance Summit? Summit has no source → provenance can never be Summit. Use explicit Summit + provenance Cuyahoga (fixture) → review; explicit Lorain + provenance Cuyahoga → review; explicit Cuyahoga + provenance Cuyahoga → live no contradiction; bare 'cuyahoga' explicit + 'cuyahoga-oh' provenance → no contradiction (base match)).\n2. Emulator integration: reconcile the same legacy property twice; stable persisted county, no duplicate tasks/events. — integration test using the Firestore emulator (is there an integration project setup in jest? Check how the integration tests spin up the emulator — the integration suite runs against the emulator per the closeout doc).\n3. Route contract test: property detail, coverage, screening, county records, and recorded documents agree — route-level test (unit-style with a fake firebase? Or integration?). The existing route tests under __tests__ use a fake harness. For contract tests of agreement across routes, an integration (emulator) test is more honest: seed a legacy property, call each route, assert the same status.\n4. Zero-write assertion for the unsupported/unknown mutation/enqueue paths — most exist (screening zero-write is tested; recorded search zero-write is tested?). Add for the new review state: a review property's search/confirm/refresh → 422 + no task/search/event written.\n\n### UI surfaces\n\n- Deep-dive coverage chip/banner (property/[id]/page.tsx) — handle 'review'.\n- Properties list pill (properties/page.tsx:778 — currently `resolveCountyCoverage(p.county ?? null, null)` — explicitly by design? The list doesn't have a county record; the same light-projection issue as /api/coverage. For consistency, the list pill should match /api/coverage... both \"light.\" Since /api/coverage becomes provenance-aware, the properties list that derives the pill client-side from p.county alone would diverge for legacy properties. Hmm — the properties page fetches the list from /api/properties (no coverage). Option: have /api/properties return per-property coverage (the list route can batch-read the county records the same way). Let me check the shape of the /api/properties response — it returns a list of WatchlistItem. Adding a per-item `coverage` field is additive. The pill uses that.\n\nOK — the scope is growing, but each piece is mechanical once the resolver semantics are set. Let me also double-check `notCoveredPayload`'s consumers and the 422 handling in the UI (deep-dive banner). I'll handle the UI during implementation.\n\n### Implementation order (WU1)\n1. capabilities.ts: add `review` status + `coverageNote` helper; update notCoveredPayload (reason 'review').\n2. types: add CoverageNote to the recorded-documents types (or reuse from capabilities — import from capabilities into types? The types module shouldn't depend on lib. Put CoverageNote in types/county-records.ts? It's a presentation projection... `src/types/county-records.ts` is the home of the domain; a client-safe projection type fits in types (RecordedSearchNotFound is there too). Add `RecordedCoverageNote` to types/recorded-documents.ts (since it's attached to recorded read responses) — or, simpler: inline a structural type in the route? No — the UI component needs it; put it in types/recorded-documents.ts.\n3. The property detail route GET+PATCH with provenance.\n4. /api/coverage provenance-aware (batched county-record reads).\n5. /api/properties list: per-item coverage (batched) — so the pill matches.\n6. Recorded-documents root + polling: guard + envelope fields.\n7. Worker: replace backfillCountyBestEffort with a transactional reconcile (new op in firestore/properties.ts); the review branch of requireLiveCounty → a distinct blocked code.\n8. UI: deep-dive banner, properties pill, recorded-documents notice, dashboard coverage card — render review.\n9. Tests: unit matrix (capabilities), route contract (integration on the emulator), zero-write (review), reconcile idempotency (unit on a fake + integration on the emulator).\n\nThat's a lot — but much of it is small. Time to write the code. Start with capabilities.ts.\n\nActually wait — one more check: the values of `WorkerBlockedError` and how the blocked code is persisted, plus the worker's retry semantics for blocked tasks (blocked = non-retriable). Let me check the codes in agent-worker + types/agent-tasks.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nDesign is settled. Before writing WU1, one last check: worker blocked-error codes and task persistence semantics.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-959d11c9afa5f7f6","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"WorkerBlockedError\\|blockedReason\\|blocked\" src/lib/agent-worker.ts | head -30; echo \"===\"; grep -n \"blocked\" src/types/agent-tasks.ts | head -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":129646,"output":4820,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4720,"totalTokens":134466,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788196904615,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8c414e9b7fc8a166","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"bcfe48e2","parentId":"e313c33b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:23:57.877Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-959d11c9afa5f7f6","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"175: * Mapped to a `blocked` task with the exact next step.\n177:export class WorkerBlockedError extends Error {\n183:    this.name = 'WorkerBlockedError';\n237:  if (error instanceof WorkerBlockedError) {\n324:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n334:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n375:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n387: * world surfaces as CountyRecordChangedError (→ blocked, not retried).\n397:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n501:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n535:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n566:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n606:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n627:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n653:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n682:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n696:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n733:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n776:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n856:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n867:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n874:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n887:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n903:    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n931:      throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n946: * it, and land the terminal/blocked/requeued state. All three outcome\n===\n40:  | 'blocked'\n49:  'blocked',\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788197037877}}
{"type":"message","id":"0a8d8626","parentId":"bcfe48e2","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:24:03.916Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me check the WorkerBlockedError class and how its code is stored (lines 175-260 of agent-worker.ts).\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-836477765071d966","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":80,"offset":170,"path":"/Users/evanhuang/brickspect/dashboard/.worktrees/records-agent-launch/src/lib/agent-worker.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":134843,"output":106,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":30,"totalTokens":134949,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788197037881,"responseId":"chatcmpl-84bff59c72b97223","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b5fb2968","parentId":"0a8d8626","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:24:03.936Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-836477765071d966","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"}\n\n/**\n * Thrown by the county handlers for conditions that will NOT improve with\n * an automatic retry (state mismatches, gone upstream, invalid input).\n * Mapped to a `blocked` task with the exact next step.\n */\nexport class WorkerBlockedError extends Error {\n  constructor(\n    readonly code: string,\n    message: string\n  ) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = 'WorkerBlockedError';\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * The worker lost ownership of its work mid-run (e.g. the task lease\n * expired and a fresh worker re-claimed and re-ran it). The task document\n * belongs to the new worker now: this worker must write NOTHING (no\n * terminal state, no record) — the new worker finalizes everything.\n */\nexport class WorkerLeaseLostError extends Error {\n  constructor(message: string) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = 'WorkerLeaseLostError';\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Categorized worker failure. `blocking` codes are the ones a retry can\n * never fix (human action or a changed world state); everything else is\n * transient and gets bounded exponential backoff until maxAttempts.\n */\nexport type WorkerFailure =\n  | { kind: 'cas_conflict'; currentStateRevision: number }\n  | {\n      kind: 'blocking';\n      info: { code: string; message: string };\n    }\n  | {\n      kind: 'transient';\n      info: { code: string; message: string };\n    };\n\n/**\n * Error codes that are permanently blocking (exact next step, no retry):\n * the request/state cannot succeed as-is until a human or a new upstream\n * observation intervenes. `source_unavailable` / `source_malformed` are\n * deliberately TRANSIENT (source flakiness retries; the backoff bounds it).\n * Task 10 adds the MCP auth/CAPTCHA/paywall codes here.\n */\nconst BLOCKING_WORKER_CODES = new Set([\n  'unsupported_county',\n  'county_changed',\n  'invalid_request',\n  'parcel_gone',\n]);\n\nexport function classifyWorkerFailure(error: unknown): WorkerFailure {\n  if (error instanceof CountyRecordChangedError) {\n    return {\n      kind: 'cas_conflict',\n      currentStateRevision: error.current.revision,\n    };\n  }\n  if (error instanceof WorkerBlockedError) {\n    return {\n      kind: 'blocking',\n      info: { code: error.code, message: error.message },\n    };\n  }\n  if (error instanceof CountyRecordsError) {\n    const info: { code: string; message: string } =\n      error.code === 'unsupported_county'\n        ? { code: 'unsupported_county', message: error.message }\n        : { code: error.code, message: error.message };\n    return BLOCKING_WORKER_CODES.has(error.code)\n      ? { kind: 'blocking', info }\n\n[956 more lines in file. Use offset=250 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788197043936}}
{"type":"message","id":"4ea68846","parentId":"b5fb2968","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:24:35.813Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`WorkerBlockedError` takes an arbitrary string code, and `classifyWorkerFailure` maps all WorkerBlockedErrors to 'blocking'. Therefore, adding a `county_review` code to WorkerBlockedError is safe — no need to modify BLOCKING_WORKER_CODES (that set is for CountyRecordsError codes).\n\nI have all the information I need. Now let me implement WU1. Order:\n\n**Step 1: capabilities.ts** — add the `review` status.\n\nMain changes:\n1. Add the following to the `CountyCoverage` union:\n```ts\n| {\n    status: 'review';\n    county: CountyIdentity;\n    eta: null;\n    source: null;\n    contradiction: { explicit: CountyIdentity; provenance: CountyIdentity };\n  }\n```\n2. Inside `resolveCountyCoverage`: when a contradiction exists (regardless of the registry status of the explicit county), return review with the explicit identity (normalized via registry lookup for display purposes).\n\nWait — one nuance: for the review branch, which identity should be normalized? Currently, in non-contradiction cases, `identity = explicit ?? provenance`, then `lookupCountyCapability(identity.code)` provides the registry name. In the review case, use the explicit identity. The explicit county might not exist in the registry at all (e.g., explicit \"Lorain\" — no, lorain-oh does exist in the registry). For a completely unknown explicit county (e.g., \"Baker\" county, no state disambiguation) + provenance Cuyahoga → contradiction → review; county display = the explicit one (registry name if it exists, otherwise the derived name). Use the same normalization path: look up the registry with the explicit code; if found, use cap.name; otherwise keep the given name.\n\n3. `notCoveredPayload`: add a 'review' case to the reason union, with a message along the lines of: \"This property's county needs review: <explicit> is set, but a confirmed parcel from <provenance> proves a different county. Until a human resolves this, county work is paused.\"\n\n4. `screeningCoverageRow`: pass through the status (add 'review' to the union of the returned status).\n\n5. New helper `coverageNote(coverage)` — a client-safe projection for the recorded documents read:\n```ts\nexport function coverageNote(c: CountyCoverage): {\n  status: CountyCoverage['status'];\n  county: { code: string; name: string } | null;\n  message: string | null;\n}\n```\nFor live → { status:'live', county, message: null }. For non-live → message from notCoveredPayload.\n\n**Step 2: types** — add to types/recorded-documents.ts:\n```ts\nexport interface RecordedCoverageNote {\n  status: 'live' | 'planned' | 'not_covered' | 'unknown' | 'review';\n  county: { code: string; name: string } | null;\n  message: string | null;\n}\nexport type RecordedSearchReadResponse = (RecordedSearch | RecordedSearchNotFound) & {\n  authoritative: boolean;\n  coverage: RecordedCoverageNote;\n};\n```\n\n**Step 3: property detail route** — GET: `getCountyRecord` + provenance. PATCH: same (read the record before computing the post-update coverage).\n\n**Step 4: /api/coverage** — batch-read county records for all watchlist items; resolve each with provenance; group.\n\n**Step 5: /api/properties** — check the current route (list) and add a coverage projection per item.\n\n**Step 6: recorded-documents root + polling** — the guard + envelope.\n\n**Step 7: worker + firestore** — the transaction for `reconcileWatchlistCounty` in firestore/properties.ts; the worker's helper `reconcileCounty` (error level, no swallowing); the `requireLiveCounty` review branch → WorkerBlockedError('county_review', ...).\n\n**Step 8: UI** — the property deep-dive page (banner), the property list (pill), the recorded documents section (notification), the dashboard coverage card. Check how coverage is currently rendered and minimally extend to review.\n\n**Step 9: tests.**\n\nAlright, let's get to it. Starting with capabilities.ts.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI've confirmed the design is sound—`WorkerBlockedError` accepts any string code, so adding a review code is straightforward. Now I'll implement WU1 starting with the capabilities module.\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-89a8a567a42f1d26","name":"todo","arguments":{"action":"update","status":"completed","id":1}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e2f9f755d0f2b50","name":"todo","arguments":{"action":"update","activeForm":"Implementing canonical county resolver + guards","status":"in_progress","id":2}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":135641,"output":1098,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":940,"totalTokens":136739,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788197043941,"responseId":"chatcmpl-94e2d8c4440e648b","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
