{"type":"message","id":"8f89eab2","parentId":"59ee1534","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:43:15.719Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The state is `Record<string, string>` (not `Record<string, ScreeningVerdict>` — I was misremembering). Also, the two load sites destructure `{ items, screening }` — I need to add `coverage` to both destructurings and state. Then, at the pill's call site (around line 797, with an offset of about +25 lines after the edit to the note interface — actually, since the note interface edit added 5 lines, the pill is currently around line 802), replace it with the note-driven value.\n\nEdits:\n1. After the screeningVerdicts state, add the `coverageByProperty` state.\n2. First load site: `const { items, screening, coverage } = await fetchWatchlist();` + `setCoverageByProperty(coverage)`.\n3. Second load site: same thing.\n4. Watch-out for catch: also `setCoverageByProperty({})`? When watchload fails, coverage is stale — clear it for consistency. Add to the catch block.\n5. Pill call site: build CountyCoverage from the note:\n\n```tsx\nconst note = coverageByProperty[p.id];\n...\n<CoveragePill\n  county={p.county ?? null}\n  coverage={noteToCoverage(note, p.county ?? null)}\n/>\n```\n\nHere noteToCoverage is a small local helper:\n```tsx\nfunction noteToCoverage(\n  note: PropertyCoverageNote | undefined,\n  county: CountyIdentity | null\n): CountyCoverage {\n  if (note) {\n    switch (note.status) {\n      case 'live':\n        return { status: 'live', county: note.county ?? county, eta: null, source: null as never };\n      ...\n    }\n  }\n  return resolveCountyCoverage(county, null);\n}\n```\n\nHmm — this is getting complex. The `live` variant requires `source: {id, label}` (non-null). The note doesn't carry sourceLabel. Ugh.\n\nAn alternative cleaner design: **change CoveragePill to accept a note directly**. The pill only uses `coverage.status` and `county.name` (from the `county` prop). Let me look at the pill's code again: it uses `county` (the prop) for the name, and `coverage.status` for the variant. `coverage.county` is not used at all in the pill! So the cleanest thing is: change the pill's prop to the note shape:\n\n```tsx\nexport function CoveragePill({ county, coverage }: {\n  county: CountyIdentity | null;\n  coverage: { status: 'live' | 'planned' | 'not_covered' | 'unknown' | 'review' };\n})\n```\n\nBut the deep-dive page passes the full CountyCoverage from the detail API — structurally that still satisfies this narrower prop type (it has status). TS's structural typing: a CountyCoverage value is assignable to `{status: union}` — yes, the extra properties are fine (since we're not passing an object literal directly, excess property checks don't apply).\n\nSo: widen the pill's prop to a minimal `CoverageStatus` shape. Keep the name `coverage`. Then the list passes the note (which has status) directly, and the deep-dive passes the full CountyCoverage (compatible).\n\nThat's clean and minimal. The deep-dive banner (CountyCoverageBanner) continues to require the full CountyCoverage (it uses county.name, eta, contradiction) — the deep-dive has that from the API.\n\nAlright, let's apply: change CoveragePill's prop type to accept a status-only object. The review branch only reads county.name — no problem.\n\nNext, the list page: the pill receives `coverage={coverageByProperty[p.id] ?? {status: 'unknown', ...}}` — a fallback for when the map is empty (an old server?) — use a fallback derived from the note: `{ status: 'unknown', county: null, message: null }`? If the server is old, the `coverage` map is undefined → fetchWatchlist returns {} → all notes are undefined → the pill falls back to client-side resolveCountyCoverage(p.county ?? null, null) (old behavior) for resilience.\n\nImplementation: at the pill's call site:\n```tsx\nconst note = coverageByProperty[p.id];\n...\ncoverage={\n  note ?? resolveCountyCoverage(p.county ?? null, null)\n}\n```\nWait — resolveCountyCoverage returns a CountyCoverage, and note is a PropertyCoverageNote — the union of both must satisfy the pill's minimal prop `{status: ...}`. CountyCoverage has status — OK; PropertyCoverageNote has status — OK. But TS: `note ?? resolveCountyCoverage(...)` is a union of both shapes — both have `status` → assignable to the minimal prop.\n\nAnd the pill's prop type: `coverage: { status: 'live' | 'planned' | 'not_covered' | 'unknown' | 'review' }`. Both shapes satisfy this.\n\nAlright, let's write the edits.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8943c590d236b25b","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  /** Latest screening verdict per propertyId (the sole verdict source). */\n  const [screeningVerdicts, setScreeningVerdicts] = useState<\n    Record<string, string>\n  >({});\n  /** Canonical per-property coverage note (the server-resolved one). */\n  const [coverageByProperty, setCoverageByProperty] = useState<\n    Record<string, PropertyCoverageNote>\n  >({});\n  const [watchError, setWatchError] = useState(false);","oldText":"  /** Latest screening verdict per propertyId (the sole verdict source). */\n  const [screeningVerdicts, setScreeningVerdicts] = useState<\n    Record<string, string>\n  >({});\n  const [watchError, setWatchError] = useState(false);"},{"newText":"  const loadWatchlist = useCallback(async () => {\n    setWatchError(false);\n    try {\n      const { items, screening, coverage } = await fetchWatchlist();\n      setWatchlist(items);\n      setScreeningVerdicts(\n        Object.fromEntries(\n          screening\n            .filter((p) => p.run)\n            .map((p) => [p.propertyId, p.run!.verdict])\n        )\n      );\n      setCoverageByProperty(coverage);\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.error('Failed to load watchlist:', err);\n      setWatchlist(null);\n      setScreeningVerdicts({});\n      setCoverageByProperty({});\n      setWatchError(true);\n    }\n  }, []);","oldText":"  const loadW