{"type":"message","id":"fc03b38e","parentId":"790f20ee","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:51:40.341Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b2ed82cd7f2c5127","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { getFirestoreDb } from '@/lib/firebase/admin';\nimport type {\n  CountyIdentity,\n  WatchlistItem,\n  WatchlistSnapshot,\n} from '@/types/records-dashboard';\n\n/**\n * Tolerant reader for the optional county identity (legacy documents have\n * no field at all): undefined/empty → null (unknown, never a default).\n */\nfunction parseCountyIdentity(raw: unknown): CountyIdentity | null {\n  if (raw == null || typeof raw !== 'object') return null;\n  const r = raw as { code?: unknown; name?: unknown };\n  const code =\n    typeof r.code === 'string' && r.code.trim() !== '' ? r.code.trim() : null;\n  const name =\n    typeof r.name === 'string' && r.name.trim() !== '' ? r.name.trim() : null;\n  if (!code) return null;\n  return { code, name: name ?? code };\n}\n\nfunction propertiesCollection(uid: string) {\n  return getFirestoreDb().collection('users').doc(uid).collection('properties');\n}\n\n/**\n * List the user's watchlist (Crexi properties they track), most recently\n * added first.\n */\n * proves the property's county — source provenance, not inference).\n * Best-effort by contract: returns false when the property is gone or\n * already has an identity (an explicit assertion is never overwritten).\n */\nexport async function backfillWatchlistCounty(\n  uid: string,\n  propertyId: string,\n  county: CountyIdentity\n): Promise<boolean> {\n  const ref = propertiesCollection(uid).doc(propertyId);\n  const doc = await ref.get();\n  if (!doc.exists) return false;\n  const existing = parseCountyIdentity(\n    (doc.data() as { county?: unknown })?.county\n  );\n  if (existing) return false;\n  await ref.set({ county }, { merge: true });\n  return true;\n}\n\n/**\n * Set (or clear, with null) a property's county identity — the explicit\n * user assertion path (PATCH /api/properties/[id]). Unlike the backfill,\n * this overwrites whatever is stored: the user's assertion is the source\n * of truth. Returns the updated item (null when the property is gone).\n */\nexport async function updateWatchlistItemCounty(\n  uid: string,\n  propertyId: string,\n  county: CountyIdentity | null\n): Promise<WatchlistItem | null> {\n  const ref = propertiesCollection(uid).doc(propertyId);\n  const doc = await ref.get();\n  if (!doc.exists) return null;\n  await ref.set(county ? { county } : { county: null }, { merge: true });\n  const after = await ref.get();\n  if (!after.exists) return null;\n  const data = after.data() as Omit<WatchlistItem, 'id'>;\n  return {\n    id: after.id,\n    ...data,\n    county: parseCountyIdentity(data.county),\n  };\n}\n\n/** Remove a property from the watchlist; false when it wasn't tracked. */\nexport async function deleteWatchlistItem(\n  uid: string,\n  crexiPropertyId: string\n): Promise<boolean> {\n  const ref = propertiesCollection(uid).doc(crexiPropertyId);\n  const doc = await ref.get();\n  if (!doc.exists) return false;\n  await ref.delete();\n  return true;\n}\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788198700341}}
{"type":"message","id":"7ca0ddf6","parentId":"fc03b38e","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:52:10.788Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I'll write `reconcileWatchlistCounty` — a transactional version that replaces the non-atomic `backfillWatchlistCounty`. Design:\n\n```ts\n/**\n * The result of reconciling a legacy county-less property against\n * provenance-derived county identity.\n */\nexport type CountyReconcileOutcome =\n  | { status: 'reconciled'; county: CountyIdentity }\n  | { status: 'already_reconciled'; county: CountyIdentity }\n  | { status: 'explicit_conflict'; county: CountyIdentity; stored: CountyIdentity }\n  | { status: 'property_gone' };\n\n/**\n * Transactional, idempotent county reconciliation (M1 closeout).\n *\n * Unlike the legacy `backfillWatchlistCounty` (read-then-write, where a\n * concurrent explicit PATCH could race and get overwritten), this runs\n * the check and the write in a single Firestore transaction:\n *\n *   - property absent          → property_gone (no write)\n *   - explicit county present  → explicit_conflict (no write) — an explicit\n *     assertion is never overwritten by provenance; the contradiction\n *     surfaces as the fail-closed review state on all read surfaces\n *   - no county, same base     → already_reconciled (no write — idempotent)\n *   - no county                → writes { county, countyReconciledFrom,\n *     countyReconciledAt } (merge) → reconciled\n *\n * Errors propagate (never swallowed): a write failure after a confirmed\n * parcel leaves the property county-less but resolvable on all surfaces\n * via provenance, and the next county work reconciles again.\n */\nexport async function reconcileWatchlistCounty(\n  uid: string,\n  propertyId: string,\n  county: CountyIdentity,\n  sourceId: string\n): Promise<CountyReconcileOutcome> {\n  const db = getFirestoreDb();\n  return db.runTransaction(async (tx) => {\n    const ref = propertiesCollection(uid).doc(propertyId);\n    const doc = await tx.get(ref);\n    if (!doc.exists) return { status: 'property_gone' as const };\n    const existing = parseCountyIdentity((doc.data() as {county?: unknown})?.county);\n    if (existing) {\n      // Same base county → nothing to do (idempotent); different →\n      // conflict, never overwrite.\n      const sameBase =\n        countyBase(normalizeCountyCode(existing.code) ?? '') ===\n        countyBase(normalizeCountyCode(county.code) ?? '');\n      return sameBase\n        ? { status: 'already_reconciled', county: existing }\n        : { status: 'explicit_conflict', county, stored: existing };\n    }\n    tx.set(\n      ref,\n      {\n        county,\n        countyReconciledFrom: sourceId,\n        countyReconciledAt: new Date().toISOString(),\n      },\n      { merge: true }\n    );\n    return { status: 'reconciled', county };\n  });\n}\n```\n\nHmm — wait. In the \"same base\" case, is it OK for the stored value to be an explicit county with a different state token (e.g., stored as 'cuyahoga' and the derived value is 'cuyahoga-oh')? The plan says \"idempotent\" — if the stored value is already 'cuyahoga' and the derived value is 'cuyahoga-oh', should we normalize it? Conservative approach: treat as already reconciled (base is the same) and don't touch it. That's honest idempotency, and normalization is not required. OK.\n\nNote: the `already_reconciled` when the stored value is the same — return the stored value (not the derived value) — it's informational.\n\nShould I keep the old `backfillWatchlistCounty`? The worker is the only caller (3 sites). The plan says to replace it with transactional reconciliation. I'll remove the old function and update the worker's calls. But wait — is `backfillWatchlistCounty` used anywhere else (tests)? I need to grep. Also, its behavior (returning bool) is different. Let me check the usages first.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d36eff8c86a40a6","name":"bash","ar