{"type":"message","id":"b89a2617","parentId":"3eb1b7bf","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:26:46.262Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Three type errors in my new test file:\n1. L53: `{ ...(state[`${uid}:${id}`] as object) ?? {} ... }` — no wait, L53 is inside the first set implementation: `{ ...((state[...] as object) ?? {}), ...data }` — spreading `data` (unknown) → error. Need to cast data: `...(data as Record<string, unknown>)`.\n2. L94: same thing with `...(state[k] as object)` — spreading state[k] (unknown) → cast it.\n3. L147: `second.county` — the outcome union type: after `expect(second.status).toBe('reconciled')`... no wait, line 147 is the second reconcile test: after `expect(second.status).toBe('already_reconciled')`, `second.county` — TypeScript doesn't narrow via expect(). Fix: `expect((second as { status: 'already_reconciled'; county: CountyIdentity }).county)` or just check with a toEqual against the whole object: `expect(second).toEqual({ status: 'already_reconciled', county: CUYAHOGA })` — but note: already_reconciled returns the \"stored\" county (which, after the first reconcile, is CUYAHOGA itself, so it's the same value). A full-object toEqual is cleaner. However, to keep the intent explicit, use a cast.\n\nAlso, there's an unused `key` function — remove it.\n\nLet's fix all of it with edits.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e2433ce69c4b602","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  const state: Record<string, unknown> = { ...seed };\n\n  const propertyDoc = (uid: string, id: string): FakeDoc => ({\n    id,\n    get: () =>\n      Promise.resolve({\n        exists: state[`${uid}:${id}`] !== undefined,\n        id,\n        data: () => state[`${uid}:${id}`],\n      }),\n    set: (data, opts) => {\n      if (opts?.merge) {\n        state[`${uid}:${id}`] = {\n          ...((state[`${uid}:${id}`] as Record<string, unknown>) ?? {}),\n          ...(data as Record<string