{"type":"message","id":"4bf22c24","parentId":"09499765","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:27:44.928Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-94a1eeb513bf8a4f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"11: *      used (Task 1 CAS + outbox events), tagged `actor: 'worker'` with\n15: *     - success        → completeTask (result: event ref + state revision\n27: *   4. Between tasks the worker also runs the domain-event projector (Task\n28: *      3): claim a pending/expired event, write its deterministic\n47:import { buildDomainEventId } from '@/lib/firestore/domain-events';\n49:  claimPendingEvent,\n50:  failEventProjection,\n51:  projectClaimedEvent,\n52:} from '@/lib/firestore/domain-event-projector';\n124:/** The aggregate type the county-records module uses for its outbox events. */\n286:  eventType: string;\n472:    return { eventType: 'county_record_resolved', record };\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788200864928}}
{"type":"message","id":"730ae394","parentId":"4bf22c24","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:27:54.138Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The event goes through the domain-events outbox (claimPendingEvent / projectClaimedEvent). The collection name is probably 'domainEvents', or it's a per-aggregate outbox. The exact name doesn't matter that much for my test: the \"zero duplicate tasks/events\" assertion can count the following:\n- Documents in users/{uid}/agentTasks\n- The documents in the event collection the worker uses — let me check what collection the domain-event projector writes to. No, actually, a simpler and still honest approach is: count the total document count under users/{uid}/ (a full snapshot of the user's subcollections before and after the second reconcile) — if the reconcile wrote any extra task/event/document at all, the snapshot will change. This is a strong zero-write assertion that doesn't depend on collection names.\n\nTo take a snapshot of all subcollections: `db.collectionGroup` requires indexes. Better approach: list known subcollections — properties, countyRecords, agentTasks, and the domain-events outbox collection. Let me quickly look for the outbox collection name.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fc2acae324157a6","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"collection(\" src/lib/firestore/domain-events.ts | head -8; grep -n \"collection(\" src/lib/firestore/domain-event-projector.ts 2>/dev/null | head -8"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":115133,"output":292,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":223,"totalTokens":115425,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788200864932,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a50504e7d8d66eb3","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"98927c8c","parentId":"730ae394","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:27:54.200Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-9fc2acae324157a6","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"85:    .collection('users')\n87:    .collection('domainEvents')\n154:          .collection('users')\n156:          .collection('domainEvents')\n159:          .collection('users')\n161:          .collection('domainEvents');\n208:    .collection('users')\n210:    .collection('feedItems')\n216:    .collection('users')\n218:    .collection('actionItems')\n257:        .collection('users')\n259:        .collection('domainEvents')\n266:        .collection('users')\n268:        .collection('domainEvents')\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788200874200}}
{"type":"message","id":"2b0eb598","parentId":"98927c8c","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:28:35.498Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nCollections under users/{uid}: properties, countyRecords (a subcollection of properties), agentTasks, domainEvents, feedItems, actionItems. The county record is at users/{uid}/properties/{pid}/countyRecords/current.\n\nNext, I'll write an integration test. The model is the recorded-documents one. Main contents:\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Integration test: transactional county reconciliation (emulator).\n * ...\n */\nimport { upsertWatchlistItem, getWatchlistItem, reconcileWatchlistCounty } from '@/lib/firestore/properties';\nimport { enqueueCountyTaskIfCurrent, getCountyRecord } from '@/lib/firestore/county-records';\nimport { processOneTask } from '@/lib/agent-worker';\nimport { getFirestoreDb } from '@/lib/firebase/admin';\n\nconst CUYAHOGA = { code: 'cuyahoga-oh', name: 'Cuyahoga County' };\nconst ADDR = { street: '5180 broadview road', unit: null, city: 'cleveland', state: 'OH', zip: '44143' };\nconst runId = () => `${Date.now()}-${Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e6)}`;\n\nasync function seedLegacyCountyless(uid, propertyId) {\n  await upsertWatchlistItem(uid, propertyId, {\n    address: '5180 Broadview Rd', city: 'Cleveland', state: 'OH', zipCode: '44143',\n    county: null,  // legacy: added before county identity existed\n    propertyType: 'residential', title: 'Broadview Rd House', crexiId: propertyId,\n    listingUrl: null, thumbnailUrl: null, price: null, sizeSqFt: null, pricePerSqFt: null,\n    capturedAt: new Date().toISOString(),\n  });\n}\n\nasync function countUserDocs(uid): Promise<number> {\n  const db = getFirestoreDb();\n  const userRef = db.collection('users').doc(uid);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const sub of ['properties', 'agentTasks', 'domainEvents', 'feedItems', 'actionItems']) {\n    const snap = await userRef.collection(sub).get();\n    total += snap.size;\n    if (sub === 'properties') {\n      // countyRecords subcollection per property\n      for (const doc of snap.docs) {\n        const cr = await doc.ref.collection('countyRecords').get();\n        total += cr.size;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nHold on — I need to check the signature of upsertWatchlistItem: (uid, propertyId, snapshot: WatchlistSnapshot) — I'll check the fields of the WatchlistSnapshot type (in the recorded-documents tests it was being used with all the fields including verdict? No — the WatchlistSnapshot there didn't have a verdict; it was a snapshot type). I'll copy that shape and make county null.\n\nTest:\n1. 'auto-confirm reconciles a legacy county-less property (durable, provenance-recorded)':\n   - Seed a legacy county-less; enqueue county_resolve; processOneTask (the fixture auto-confirms: resolve→r2? In the recorded-documents tests, confirmParcel returns 2 after a single processOneTask call — the fixture auto-confirms within the same task run).\n   - Actually — wait: in the recorded-documents tests, processOneTask(enq.task) handles county_resolve, and the fixture auto-confirms internally (one task → resolve + auto-confirm, revisions r1+r2). So after processOneTask: the county record is confirmed (r2), and the worker reconciles the property's county.\n   - Assert: getWatchlistItem → county equals CUYAHOGA; the raw document has countyReconciledFrom 'cuyahoga-fixture' (what's the source id for fixture mode? The fixture provider's source id — check: the Cuyahoga fixture source id is probably 'cuyahoga-fixture'; the live one is 'cuyahoga-arcgis'. In fixture mode, the snapshot's provenance.sourceId is — check the provider/fixtures). I'll assert that countyReconciledFrom is a string starting with 'cuyahoga' (or match it exactly after confirming the fixture source id).\n2. 'reconcile is idempotent (no duplicate tasks/events on re-attempt)':\n   - After step 1 (or as a standalone test: seed, auto-confirm, record the count of user documents, call reconcileWatchlistCounty directly → already_reconciled; assert that the count of user documents is unchanged and that the county is unchanged.\n   - Each test is independent (a unique uid per test).\n3. 'explicit county conflict is never overwritten':\n   - Seed a property with county summit-oh and a confirmed Cuyahoga snapshot (seed the county record directly with a confirmed status + a Cuyahoga snapshot; or enqueue resolve — the fixture will auto-confirm Cuyahoga).\n   - Then run the refresh task → requireLiveCounty throws county_review → the task is blocked (status failed/blocked, code county_review) with zero writes to the county record.\n   - Assert: the property's county is still summit-oh, the county record is unchanged, the task status is blocked with code 'county_review'.\n\nHmm — test 3 overlaps with the \"zero writes on review\" requirement, but this is the worker-level behavior. It has value: it proves that review blocks the worker, not just the routes.\n\nAlso 'a zero-county property without a confirmed snapshot is not backfilled' — the fixture auto-confirms, so a confirmed snapshot always exists after resolve; to test the no-provenance case I need a county-less property with no snapshot — but then no county work runs in the first place (live is required)… this is a trivial matter: if there's no snapshot, resolveCountyCoverage = unknown → the worker is blocked. That's already covered by the unit tests. Skip.\n\nI'll check the fixture provider's source id and the shape of the county record's confirmed snapshot (getCountyRecord returns a StoredCountyRecord with a snapshot). Let me check the fixture source id in fixtures.ts.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-92ea8916aaf84047","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"sourceId\\|source\" src/lib/county-records/fixtures.ts | head -8; grep -n \"sourceId\" src/lib/county-records/cuyahoga-arcgis.ts | head -5; grep -n