{"type":"message","id":"9591bb54","parentId":"0d4a2c27","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:32:59.252Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b8d836089eefb196","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAIL integration __tests__/integration/firestore/county-reconcile.integration.test.ts\n  ● county reconciliation (emulator) › auto-confirmed parcel durably reconciles a legacy county-less property\n  ● county reconciliation (emulator) › reconciliation is idempotent: a re-attempt writes nothing new\n  ● county reconciliation (emulator) › a review contradiction blocks worker county work with zero writes\nTest Suites: 1 failed, 1 total\nTests:       3 failed, 3 total\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788201179252}}
{"type":"message","id":"b972f800","parentId":"9591bb54","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:33:23.142Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nThe advisory is just pointing out a pattern, no action needed. All three integration tests failed — to understand the cause, I need the actual failure details:\n\n1. Test 1: `expect(received).toEqual(CUYAHOGA)` — the county doesn't match {code:'cuyahoga-oh', name:'Cuyahoga County'}. Probably the reconciled county's name differs (the registry name is 'Cuyahoga County' — it should match), or the reconcile wrote a different identity, or the auto-confirm didn't happen (the fixture's search for that address returned something different → no confirmation → no reconcile).\n\n2. Test 2: toBe failure — probably a count mismatch (countUserDocs before/after differs — could be that domainEvents or feedItems are being written on some delayed path, or my counting is missing a subcollection where writes occur, or the second reconcile actually wrote something).\n\n3. Test 3: `expect(recordBefore).not.toBeNull()` — after auto-confirm, getCountyRecord returns null?! This suggests that the auto-confirm in tests 2/3 didn't actually persist a confirmed record — the same root cause as test 1.\n\nCommon root cause hypothesis: the fixture provider's search for '5180 broadview road' doesn't return an exact match for this new property (the recorded-documents suite used the same address with that provider — but the COUNTY fixture provider might match by a different key), or the worker run failed at an earlier stage (e.g. requireLiveCounty against a county-less property: resolveCountyCoverage(null, null) = 'unknown' → the worker throws unsupported_county before searching!). That's it — that's exactly it. For a county-less property, the worker's requireLiveCounty → coverage 'unknown' → WorkerBlockedError('unsupported_county') → blocked. In other words, the county work for a county-less property is gated by requireLiveCounty itself — the worker can't run county_resolve on an unknown-county property at all!\n\nWait — but then, how does a county-less legacy property ever get its county in the first place? If the worker requires live coverage before running resolve, a county-less property (unknown) can never be resolved → provenance backfill never happens → the \"provenance backfill for legacy properties\" feature is dead at the worker level!\n\nHmm, hold on — let me re-read requireLiveCounty: `resolveCountyCoverage(item.county ?? null, record?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId ?? null)`. For a county-less property with no record: (null, null) → unknown → blocked. So yes — county_resolve is blocked for a county-less property. This looks like a design flaw: the purpose of resolve is to establish the county for properties whose county is unknown (the fixture resolves Cuyahoga from the address, and the confirmed snapshot backfills the county). If the gate blocks unknown, the only path to a county for a county-less property is a manual user PATCH.\n\nLet me check what the original pre-audit behavior was — was there an exception in requireLiveCounty for the resolve kind? Let me look at the current requireLiveCounty in agent-worker (lines 314-360) and how runCountyResolve calls it. Maybe there's a special case: for 'county_resolve', allow unknown/unknown-county and let the fixture resolve. Let me read runCountyResolve.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ea392b9bfcf3a4d","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '314,363p' src/lib/agent-worker.ts; echo ===RUNRESOLVE===; sed -n '423,470p' src/lib/agent-worker.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":131215,"output":805,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":732,"totalTokens":132020,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788201179257,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8fd2f6ff8add49fc","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"78ac31bb","parentId":"b972f800","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:33:23.192Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-9ea392b9bfcf3a4d","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"async function requireLiveCounty(\n  item: WatchlistItem,\n  record: StoredCountyRecord | null,\n  expectedCounty: CountyIdentity | null | undefined\n): Promise<LiveCoverage> {\n  const coverage = resolveCountyCoverage(\n    item.county ?? null,\n    record?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId ?? null\n  );\n  if (coverage.status === 'review') {\n    // Fail closed: the explicit county and a confirmed parcel disagree.\n    // A distinct code — this is not \"the county is unsupported\"; it is\n    // a contradiction only a human can resolve.\n    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n      'county_review',\n      notCoveredPayload(coverage).message\n    );\n  }\n  if (coverage.status !== 'live') {\n    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n      'unsupported_county',\n      notCoveredPayload(coverage).message\n    );\n  }\n  if (\n    expectedCounty &&\n    normalizeCountyCode(expectedCounty.code) !==\n      normalizeCountyCode(coverage.county.code)\n  ) {\n    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n      'county_changed',\n      'The property\\u2019s county changed since this work was queued. Reload and re-run it.'\n    );\n  }\n  return coverage;\n}\n\n/**\n * Reconcile a legacy county-less property against the county proven by\n * the parcel just confirmed/refreshed (the ONLY county-write path for\n * non-explicit counties).\n *\n * The domain action (the CAS parcel write) has ALREADY committed by the\n * time this runs, so a genuine persistence failure here must not roll\n * anything back and must not fail the task — but it is NOT swallowed\n * either: it is logged at error level with the identifiers a recovery\n * needs, and the reconciliation is idempotent, so the next county work\n * re-attempts it. Until it lands, every surface still agrees through\n * provenance (the confirmed snapshot proves the county).\n */\n===RUNRESOLVE===\nasync function runCountyResolve(\n  task: AgentTask,\n  ctx: WorkerContext\n): Promise<TaskSuccess> {\n  // SAFETY: the county resolve route builds this exact shape at enqueue\n  // time (sanitized: address from the watchlist, no PII, bounded).\n  const input = task.input as unknown as CountyResolveInput;\n  if (!input.address || typeof input.address.state !== 'string') {\n    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n      'invalid_input',\n      'This task is missing its address; re-run the resolution from the property page.'\n    );\n  }\n  const uid = task.ownerUid;\n  const propertyId = task.propertyId;\n  const item = await requireWatchlistProperty(uid, propertyId);\n  const address = input.address;\n\n  // M1 coverage gate: re-verify county capability at run time (the world\n  // may have changed since enqueue) and require the captured county to\n  // still match. Fails closed — no county writes, no provider call, no\n  // state-based defaulting to Cuyahoga.\n  const record = await getCountyRecord(uid, propertyId);\n  const coverage = await requireLiveCounty(item, record, input.county);\n\n  const provider = providerFor(ctx, coverage.county);\n  const candidates = await provider.resolveCandidates(\n    address,\n    scenarioOption(ctx)\n  );\n  const classification = classifyParcelCandidates(address, candidates);\n\n  if (classification.result === 'no_match') {\n    const record = await saveResolution(\n      uid,\n      propertyId,\n      {\n        status: 'no_match',\n        resolvedAddress: address,\n        candidates: null,\n        lastRefresh: {\n          at: new Date(ctx.now?.() ?? Date.now()).toISOString(),\n          ok: true,\n          error: null,\n        },\n        expectedRevision: input.expectedRevision,\n      },\n      casOptionsFor(task)\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788201203192}}
{"type":"message","id":"bf2b3753","parentId":"78ac31bb","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:33:46.577Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nConfirming the root cause: `runCountyResolve` calls `requireLiveCounty(item, record, input.county)` and requires live coverage before the provider call. For a property with no county (unknown), this throws `unsupported_county` → the task gets blocked. In other words, in the current design, county resolution cannot run for properties without a county — meaning my \"auto-confirm against a legacy property with no county\" test scenario is impossible by design, and the provenance-backfill path I built can only fire for properties that have an explicit county... wait, then when would a property with no county ever get a confirmed snapshot that the reconcile would pick up? Only if the user explicitly patches the county first (in which case the county is no longer missing — the reconcile early-returns via `if (item.county) return;`).\n\nHold on — that would make the reconcile function dead code. Except: the coverage gate has to permit `unknown` for resolve (the purpose of resolve is to discover the county for properties without a county!). Let me think about what the correct design is:\n\n- `county_resolve`: the purpose is to identify the county's parcel. If the property has an explicit county → that county must be live (otherwise you can't call that provider). If the property has no county... in the original design (pre-audit, M1), the gate for a county-less property was — hmm. The fixture provider is Cuyahoga-specific. A county-less property with a Cuyahoga address: the fixture resolves by address. The M1 design philosophy: \"never infer the county from state or address\" — but the fixture provider's address resolution is a search against the Cuyahoga layer, and if it finds an exact match, that parcel is in Cuyahoga (provenance from the source, not inference from address). So allowing resolve for a county-less property is legitimate only when a county source can be tried — but without a county, which provider do we choose? There's no way to know the county from the address (that's the inference we banned)...\n\nHowever: the only live source is Cuyahoga. If we try the only live provider and it returns an exact-match parcel for the property's address, the parcel's provenance proves Cuyahoga. Is that \"inference from address\"? The M1 gate says \"no state-based default to Cuyahoga\" — defaulting = assuming Cuyahoga without a source. Actually querying the source and getting back a matched parcel is provenance, not inference.\n\nBut what did the original system (the audited commit) do? That's the key question — did the audited system permit resolve for a county-less property? Let me check the git diff for requireLiveCounty — I modified it (added a review branch), but was the base behavior (unknown → block) present in the audited commit? Let me look at the audited version of runCountyResolve/requireLiveCounty.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9db0311b45043748","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"git diff a9eeff99 -- src/lib/agent-worker.ts | head -60"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":132740,"output":681,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":634,"totalTokens":133421,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788201203196,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b3c67296770c2a95","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"2554796b","parentId":"bf2b3753","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:33:46.627Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-9db0311b45043748","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"diff --git a/src/lib/agent-worker.ts b/src/lib/agent-worker.ts\nindex 0de52a2..52dde30 100644\n--- a/src/lib/agent-worker.ts\n+++ b/src/lib/agent-worker.ts\n@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@ import {\n   startRecordedSearch,\n } from '@/lib/firestore/recorded-documents';\n import { getWatchlistItem } from '@/lib/firestore/properties';\n-import { backfillWatchlistCounty } from '@/lib/firestore/properties';\n+import { reconcileWatchlistCounty } from '@/lib/firestore/properties';\n import { classifyParcelCandidates } from '@/lib/county-records/address';\n import {\n   notCoveredPayload,\n@@ -320,6 +320,15 @@ async function requireLiveCounty(\n     item.county ?? null,\n     record?.snapshot?.provenance?.sourceId ?? null\n   );\n+  if (coverage.status === 'review') {\n+    // Fail closed: the explicit county and a confirmed parcel disagree.\n+    // A distinct code — this is not \"the county is unsupported\"; it is\n+    // a contradiction only a human can resolve.\n+    throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n+      'county_review',\n+      notCoveredPayload(coverage).message\n+    );\n+  }\n   if (coverage.status !== 'live') {\n     throw new WorkerBlockedError(\n       'unsupported_county',\n@@ -339,18 +348,43 @@ async function requireLiveCounty(\n   return coverage;\n }\n \n-/** Best-effort: persist the county on a legacy item once confirmed. */\n-async function backfillCountyBestEffort(\n+/**\n+ * Reconcile a legacy county-less property against the county proven by\n+ * the parcel just confirmed/refreshed (the ONLY county-write path for\n+ * non-explicit counties).\n+ *\n+ * The domain action (the CAS parcel write) has ALREADY committed by the\n+ * time this runs, so a genuine persistence failure here must not roll\n+ * anything back and must not fail the task — but it is NOT swallowed\n+ * either: it is logged at error level with the identifiers a recovery\n+ * needs, and the reconciliation is idempotent, so the next county work\n+ * re-attempts it. Until it lands, every surface still agrees through\n+ * provenance (the confirmed snapshot proves the county).\n+ */\n+async function reconcileCountyBestEffort(\n   uid: string,\n   item: WatchlistItem,\n-  county: CountyIdentity\n+  coverage: LiveCoverage\n ): Promise<void> {\n-  if (item.county) return;\n+  if (item.county) return; // an explicit identity never gets touched\n   try {\n-    await backfillWatchlistCounty(uid, item.id, county);\n+    const outcome = await reconcileWatchlistCounty(\n+      uid,\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788201226627}}
{"type":"message","id":"31c9f49b","parentId":"2554796b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:34:10.433Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nIn the audited baseline, `requireLiveCounty` behaves the same way with respect to unknown (the `coverage.status !== 'live'` throw existed as of a9eeff9 — the diff only shows my review branch being added above it). In other words, the audited system was already blocking county resolution against properties without a county — the \"auto-confirm on legacy properties without a county\" flow was never executable at the worker level to begin with. The old `backfillWatchlistCounty` (best-effort) also only ran after live coverage + a successful parcel confirmation — so in the audited world, a property without a county would never reach that code either (requireLiveCounty blocks unknown). In fact, the backfill was effectively nearly dead for properties without a county — the only path where both \"live coverage\" and \"item.county is null\" hold simultaneously is: a property without a county that has a confirmed snapshot whose provenance resolves to a live county (e.g., a legacy property where the parcel was confirmed in the old days before the M1 gate existed, or a seed data case). In that case, resolution/refresh passes (coverage is live via provenance) and the backfill writes the county. That's the real legacy scenario: a property without a county that already has a confirmed Cuyahoga snapshot (seeded or created before the gate existed).\n\nTherefore, the test scenario should be as follows: a property without a county + a confirmed Cuyahoga snapshot seeded directly (a legacy record from before M1). In that case:\n- coverage = live (provenance) → resolution/refresh is allowed → the fixture's provider operates on the Cuyahoga county.\n- Test 1: seed a property without a county + a confirmed snapshot (seed the record document directly via raw write — legitimate for simulating legacy state; this is exactly the \"current seed doesn't have one\" case mentioned in the plan!). Then run county_refresh (or resolve) → the worker reconciles the county → assert it's persisted.\n- Wait — the plan says: \"Emulator integration test that creates a legacy property without a county with a confirmed Cuyahoga snapshot (the current seed lacks one), reconciles twice, and asserts stable county and zero duplicate tasks/events.\" Yes — seed the legacy state (property without a county + confirmed snapshot), then execute reconciliation (via a county operation that runs successfully, or directly), twice.\n\nRegarding seeding a confirmed record document: we need the exact document format that normalizeStoredRecord expects. From the StoredCountyRecord type: schemaVersion, ownerUid, revision, createdAt, status, resolvedAddress, candidates, snapshot, unsupportedReason, lastRefresh, ... (there may be more fields — snapshotConfirmedAt? provenance?). Let me read the full StoredCountyRecord type and normalizeStoredRecord, and further check how the confirm path writes it (saveConfirmation) and reproduce the format exactly. A better approach: use the actual `saveConfirmation`/`saveResolution` functions in the test to create the record — these are real seams with CAS+events. But saveResolution takes expectedRevision 0 and saves status 'confirmation_required' with candidates; then saveConfirmation(uid, propertyId, {parcelId..., expectedRevision:1}) → confirmed r2 with snapshot. This is the real flow, minus the provider search! Let me check the signatures of saveResolution/saveConfirmation and the fields of the candidate/parcel shape.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb6218f17405306b","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '192,240p' src/types/county-records.ts; echo ===; grep -n \"export async function saveConfirmation\" -A 30 src/lib/firestore/county-records.ts | head -40"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":134106,"output":809,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":729,"totalTokens":134915,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788201226631,"responseId":"chatcmpl-8481e1e075bf8aa7","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"076a3b97","parentId":"31c9f49b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:34:10.491Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-bb6218f17405306b","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"export interface StoredCountyRecord {\n  schemaVersion: typeof COUNTY_RECORDS_SCHEMA_VERSION;\n  /**\n   * Denormalized owner (the document always lives under `users/{uid}`;\n   * server consumers must never re-derive identity from the path alone).\n   * Backfilled from the path on the first CAS write of a legacy doc.\n   */\n  ownerUid: string;\n  /**\n   * Monotonic optimistic-concurrency revision. Legacy (pre-CAS) documents\n   * carry no field and READ as 0; the first CAS transition bumps them to 1.\n   * Every committed transition (success or failure) bumps by exactly 1,\n   * atomically with the record write and its canonical outbox event.\n   */\n  revision: number;\n  /** When this record was first stored (ISO). Legacy: backfilled from `updatedAt`. */\n  createdAt: string;\n  status: StoredCountyRecordStatus;\n  /** The normalized address last resolved (drift detection). */\n  resolvedAddress: NormalizedPropertyAddress | null;\n  /** Pending candidates (only in confirmation_required). Bounded. */\n  candidates: StoredCandidate[] | null;\n  /** Last successfully retrieved snapshot (last-known-good). */\n  snapshot: CountyParcelSnapshot | null;\n  unsupportedReason: 'state_not_supported' | 'provider_unsupported' | null;\n  lastRefresh: {\n    at: string;\n    ok: boolean;\n    error: CountyRecordsErrorInfo | null;\n  } | null;\n  updatedAt: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * Effective status the API/UI renders, derived from StoredCountyRecord\n * (the API never returns 'loading' — that is a client-only transient):\n * - absent record → not_searched\n * - confirmation_required / no_match / unsupported_county → same\n * - confirmed + snapshot: last refresh failed → refresh_failed;\n *   snapshot stale → stale; otherwise loaded\n * - source_error without a snapshot → source_unavailable\n */\nexport type CountyRecordsStatus =\n  | 'not_searched'\n  | 'confirmation_required'\n  | 'no_match'\n  | 'loaded'\n  | 'stale'\n  | 'refresh_failed'\n===\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788201250491}}
