{"type":"message","id":"13dd88e7","parentId":"6b4df6cf","timestamp":"2026-08-31T18:52:50.751Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Revision is 4, not 3. Why? The re-resolution ran the worker's resolve: the worker's runCountyResolve flow in the auto-confirm case: it probably writes the resolution (r3: confirmation_required with the new candidates) and then auto-confirms (r4). That is, the worker's auto-confirm path first persists the resolution (saveResolution) and then confirms (confirmCountyParcel) — that's 2 CAS transitions: r2 → r3 (resolution) → r4 (confirmation). My prediction of 3 had assumed the direct-confirm case from r2. Both transitions are correct behavior (the last-known-good and candidates get updated at resolution time, and then the confirmation). The events: the resolution probably doesn't emit an event (saveResolution — does it emit 'county_record_resolved'? Let's not guess — the event assertion at line 230 will tell us: eventsBefore+1 or +2).\n\nFix: expect revision 4, and for the event count, assert the exact observed value: check what saveResolution writes as an event. From the code I read: saveResolution's casTransition — the eventType of the write? In the snippet I saw, for the confirmation_required case it returned `{kind:'write', record, eventType: ...}` — I didn't see the event name. Empirically, I'll run it, and if it's +2, assert +2; or, more simply, assert that it's greater than eventsBefore and at most 2… no — an honest test should be exact. Let me read the event name in saveResolution's write branch and the auto-confirm section of the worker.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba5409bdff295503","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '400,424p' src/lib/firestore/county-records.ts; echo ===AUTOCONFIRM===; sed -n '545,600p' src/lib/agent-worker.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":85150,"output":419