{"type":"message","id":"50cb9be5","parentId":"f0cfe844","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:31:18.118Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nThe plan file's heading is not \"WU3\". Let me try searching for the Copilot reclaim section by a different method.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a9440c655c086d3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -n -i \"copilot\\|reclaim\" docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-23-records-agent-final-audit-gap-closure.md | head -20; echo ---; grep -n \"^#\\|^##\" docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-23-records-agent-final-audit-gap-closure.md | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":92344,"output":158,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":42,"totalTokens":92502,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788204671821,"responseId":"chatcmpl-94bf92e8a8cd9090","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"32e4a084","parentId":"50cb9be5","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:31:18.174Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-9a9440c655c086d3","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5:Close the nine verified discrepancies remaining after `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-23-records-agent-post-audit-completion.md`, then reconcile closeout evidence against one exact commit. Preserve county fail-closed behavior, explicit Copilot confirmation, durable audits, fixture honesty, product deferrals, and all existing checks.\n63:## Work unit 3 — Make Copilot stale reclaim atomic and redact identifiers\n67:- Replace read-then-unconditional-update reclaim in `src/lib/copilot-actions.ts` with a Firestore transaction/CAS.\n77:- Emulator-backed concurrent reclaim test using a barrier: two post-grace confirmations, exactly one domain action and one audit lifecycle.\n162:- Browser test: streamed Copilot proposal card appears before reload and survives reload.\n167:- Omnibox accessible-name and Copilot-only behavior test.\n168:- Concurrent Copilot reclaim integration test from Work unit 3.\n191:12. Concurrent Copilot reclaim rehearsal.\n200:2. Copilot atomic reclaim/redaction.\n207:Use focused agents for independent units. Keep county reconciliation and Copilot transaction work in high-reasoning lanes. Require each implementer to report changed files, exact tests/results, assumptions, and residual risk. Independently verify actual diffs and results before accepting handoffs.\n213:- Concurrent stale Copilot retries dispatch exactly one action and audit inputs are safely transformed.\n218:- Seed-twice, zero-write, browser, mobile, provenance, and concurrent-reclaim evidence is committed and reproducible.\n---\n1:# Records Agent Final Audit-Gap Closure Plan\n3:## Objective\n7:## Starting point\n17:## Work unit 1 — Canonical legacy county reconciliation\n19:### Implementation\n31:### Tests\n38:### Acceptance\n42:## Work unit 2 — Complete provenance on feed and task surfaces\n44:### Implementation\n53:### Tests\n59:### Acceptance\n63:## Work unit 3 — Make Copilot stale reclaim atomic and redact identifiers\n65:### Implementation\n75:### Tests\n81:### Acceptance\n85:## Work unit 4 — Finish light-only theme enforcement\n87:### Implementation\n93:### Tests\n99:### Acceptance\n103:## Work unit 5 — Correct screening column semantics\n105:### Implementation\n111:### Tests\n116:### Acceptance\n120:## Work unit 6 — Make local-stack model configuration deterministic\n122:### Implementation\n129:### Tests\n134:### Acceptance\n138:## Work unit 7 — Repair API/runbook documentation and seed evidence\n140:### Implementation\n148:### Tests\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788204678174}}
{"type":"message","id":"f6dd87e3","parentId":"32e4a084","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:31:26.017Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nThe automated check is an advisory only (a prettier formatting pattern in capabilities.ts — but prettier --write has already been applied, so this advisory is outdated). No action needed.\n\nNext, I'll read the WU3 section (L63-84) of the plan.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-90b8ef6fa6103989","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":25,"offset":63,"path":"/Users/evanhuang/brickspect/dashboard/.worktrees/records-agent-launch/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-23-records-agent-final-audit-gap-closure.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":93135,"output":171,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":75,"totalTokens":93306,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788204678177,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a6994a0173c5d31b","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"65e406c8","parentId":"f6dd87e3","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:31:26.032Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-90b8ef6fa6103989","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Work unit 3 — Make Copilot stale reclaim atomic and redact identifiers\n\n### Implementation\n\n- Replace read-then-unconditional-update reclaim in `src/lib/copilot-actions.ts` with a Firestore transaction/CAS.\n- Compare execution state, lease timestamp/version, and attempt count before transitioning to `executing`.\n- Exactly one concurrent caller may acquire an expired execution; losers re-read and return interrupted/replay without dispatch.\n- Preserve terminal replay and fresh in-flight behavior.\n- Stop persisting caller-controlled `targetId` and `idempotencyKey` verbatim. Store validated canonical identifiers or keyed hashes sufficient for traceability/idempotency.\n- Version the execution schema if required and remain backward-compatible.\n- Document crash boundaries where domain mutation and terminal audit transition cannot be atomic.\n\n### Tests\n\n- Emulator-backed concurrent reclaim test using a barrier: two post-grace confirmations, exactly one domain action and one audit lifecycle.\n- Failure injection before claim, after claim/before dispatch, and after dispatch/before terminal audit write.\n- Sequential replay, redaction, stale revision, cross-user, malformed proposal, and audit-unavailable tests.\n\n### Acceptance\n\nConcurrent retries cannot duplicate domain effects, and durable audit records contain only validated or irreversibly transformed identifiers.\n\n## Work unit 4 — Finish light-only theme enforcement\n\n### Implementation\n\n[133 more lines in file. Use offset=88 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788204686032}}
{"type":"message","id":"80b7500e","parentId":"65e406c8","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:32:24.642Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nWU3's acceptance criteria are clear. Before implementing, since this ripples through the dispatcher's core contract, I should consider requesting a deeper reasoning pass. WU1 has already received a deeper reasoning. WU3's transactional reclaim is also a subtle concurrency design. Let me request one.\n\nActually, let me first think about what the design should be:\n\n**Design: transactional claim**\n\n```ts\nasync function claimExecution(uid, req): Promise<...> {\n  const db = getFirestoreDb();\n  const docRef = executionDocRef(db, uid, fingerprint);\n  \n  // First, a cheap read outside the transaction to avoid spinning up a transaction on the common path\n  // (replay / in-progress cases don't need a transaction).\n  const got = await docRef.get();\n  const initial = got.exists ? (got.data() as CopilotActionExecution) : null;\n  if (initial && isTerminal(initial)) return { kind: 'replay', result: replayResult(initial) };\n  if (initial && isFreshInFlight(initial)) return { kind: 'interrupted', result: {...} };\n  \n  // Only the claim/reclaim path needs atomicity:\n  let outcome: { kind: 'proceed' } | { kind: 'replay'... } | { kind: 'interrupted'... } | { kind: 'unavailable' } | null = null;\n  \n  await db.runTransaction(async (tx) => {\n    const t = await tx.get(docRef);\n    const record = t.exists ? (t.data() as CopilotActionExecution) : null;\n    const decision = decide(record);\n    switch (decision.kind) {\n      case 'fresh':\n        tx.set(docRef, { ...newRecord, state: 'accepted', attempts: 1 }, { merge: true }); // or create? \n        outcome = { kind: 'proceed' };\n        break;\n      case 'replay':\n        outcome = { kind: 'replay', result: decision.result };\n        break;\n      case 'interrupted':\n        outcome = { kind: 'interrupted', result: decision.result };\n        break;\n      case 'reclaim':\n        // CAS-style guard: only update if the document still matches what we saw (its updatedAt + state + attempts)\n        const current = t.data() as CopilotActionExecution;\n        tx.update(docRef, {\n          state: 'executing',\n          updatedAt: nowIso(),\n          attempts: (current.attempts ?? 1) + 1,\n        });\n        // Firestore's transaction provides isolation: if a concurrent transaction has already\n        // modified the document, this write will fail the commit with a conflict and retry.\n        outcome = { kind: 'proceed' };\n        break;\n    }\n  });\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThe key mechanism: Firestore transactions have snapshot isolation — if two concurrent transactions both read the same stale non-terminal document and both try to update it, the second commit will fail with a transaction-already-committed conflict and be retried (default 6 times). On retry, the second transaction re-reads and sees the first transaction's `executing` + fresh updatedAt → `isFreshInFlight` is true → `interrupted`. So exactly one winner. This satisfies \"exactly one concurrent caller can acquire an expired execution. The loser re-reads and returns interrupted/replay without dispatching.\"\n\nFor the 'fresh' case: use `tx.set(docRef, {...}, { merge: true })` inside the transaction? Or create? If we use `tx.create`, then two racing fresh claimants: the first transaction commits the create; the second's create fails with already-exists → the transaction aborts → the retry re-reads and sees the new in-flight record → interrupted. This works, and preserves the \"create wins\" semantics. But note: `tx.create` inside a retry loop — Firestore's admin SDK transaction automatically retries on abort (up to maxAttempts, default 6). The retry calls our closure again, re-reads, and re-decides. Good.\n\nEdge case: the transaction closure must be pure with respect to side effects — decide() is pure, no problem.\n\nAlso `recordGateOutcome` — the plan says to replace \"the read-then-unconditional-update reclaim\" — recordGateOutcome also has a non-atomic stale settle path (docRef.update on a stale non-terminal). To make all claim transitions atomic, we should route recordGateOutcome through the same transactional primitive: inside a transaction, read, and if a terminal record → replay (no write); fresh in-flight → interrupted (no write); stale/nonexistent → write terminal failed with the gate's code. If the write fails after retries → audit_unavailable.\n\n**Identifier redaction**\n\n- `targetId`: caller-controlled string (max 200). What is \"a validated canonical identifier\"? Per action type, the target is: a watchlist property id, an agent task id, or an owner request id. These are document ids — internal identifiers, not PII. The audit concern is persisting *verbatim caller input* — e.g., an attacker could put arbitrary text (including emails or URLs) into targetId, which gets stored in the audit record. Fix: don't store the raw targetId; store a keyed hash. But \"a validated canonical identifier or keyed hash, sufficient for traceability / idempotency.\"\n\n  The most honest, minimal approach: **HMAC-sha256 the targetId with a server-side secret** and store `targetHash` (plus, for the county action type, the targetId is a property id, and we could validate it against the watchlist and store it — but the dispatch layer already validates ownership before the domain action; the *audit record* is created before dispatch (claim is step 2, dispatch is step 4). At claim time we haven't validated the target yet (that happens in dispatchAction). So at claim time, targetId cannot be \"validated\" without moving validation earlier — which would change the ordering of the eligibility checks. The simplest, compliant approach: at claim time, store `targetRef: sha256Hex(`${uid}:${fingerprint}:${targetId}`)` — a keyed (per-user + per-fingerprint) hash that is irreversibly transformed (preimage-resistant) and still traceable for correlation (the same target always yields the same hash for the same user). Plus, for the send path, keep the derived `idempotencyKey`...\n\n- `idempotencyKey`: the caller sends the UI message id (8–200 chars). This is stored as-is. Same treatment: store a keyed hash `keyRef: sha256Hex(`${uid}:${fingerprint}:${idempotencyKey}`)`. Note: the *domain* idempotency key (e.g., `copilot:send:...`) is derived from the proposal inside dispatchAction and passed to the owner-request route — that is not what's stored in the audit record... wait, actually, does the owner-request send route persist its own idempotencyKey in its own send-attempt record? Possibly. The WU3 audit gap is specifically about the copilotActionExecutions record (\"stop persisting caller-controlled targetId and idempotencyKey as-is\"). We'll transform both on the audit record.\n\n  Also, the `action` field stores the action type (a value from the allowlist — safe, an enum). The `message` field is already redacted by redactSensitive. `fingerprint` is a sha256 — safe. `code` is an enum — safe.\n\n**schemaVersion**: add `schemaVersion: 2` (bumping because the shape changed: targetId → targetRef, idempotencyKey → keyRef). \"Version the execution schema as needed, maintaining backward compatibility.\" Backward compatibility: existing documents in the wild lack schemaVersion and have targetId — replay/isFreshInFlight must still work for old documents. Since isTerminal/isFreshInFlight depend only on state/updatedAt, old documents work. The CopilotActionExecution interface should be updated to make targetId/idempotencyKey optional (legacy documents may have them) and add schemaVersion: number. In tests we'll verify that old documents are still replayed.\n\nHmm wait — but does removing targetId from the *new* record break traceability? A keyed hash is sufficient per the audit (\"...or a keyed hash sufficient for traceability / idempotency\"). I'll name them `targetRef` and `keyRef` with a comment.\n\n**Documenting the crash boundary**: the module header comment already documents that a crash between accepted and succeeded leaves a reclaimable record; I should add an explicit note that a domain mutation and the terminal audit transition cannot be made atomic (different collections, possibly different seams), and that reconciliation relies on idempotency/CAS at the seam. The header already says much of this; I'll add a concise \"crash boundary\" note.\n\nNow, the tests in `src/lib/__tests__/copilot-actions.test.ts` — I need to read them to see the fake harness. Also the WU3 test list: a concurrent-reclaim test with a barrier (emulator-based! \"an emulator-based concurrent-reclaim test using a barrier: two confirmations after the grace period, exactly one domain action, one audit lifecycle\") — that goes into `__tests__/integration/firestore/`. Plus unit tests: failure injection (before claim, after claim / before dispatch, after dispatch / before terminal audit), sequential replay, redaction, stale revision, cross-user, malformed proposal, audit unavailable.\n\nLet me read the existing copilot-actions test file to check the harness.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nThe advisory is stale (prettier already fixed). Now let me read the existing test harness for `copilot-actions`:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb3a9c3b4ddae7b1","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"wc -l src/lib/__tests__/copilot-actions.test.ts; grep -n \"describe(\\|  it(\\|fake\\|jest.mock\\|function \" src/lib/__tests__/copilot-actions.test.ts | head -50"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":93623,"output":2198,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2096,"totalTokens":95821,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788204686036,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9ba3cfa9245bfdb3","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
