{"type":"message","id":"c17c6d56","parentId":"6a40fff9","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:32:24.713Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-bb3a9c3b4ddae7b1","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1021\n45:// --- fakes -------------------------------------------------------------------\n63:function createFakeDb(over: FakeOptions = {}) {\n116:jest.mock('@/lib/firebase/admin', () => ({\n119:jest.mock('@/lib/firestore/county-records', () => ({\n123:jest.mock('@/lib/firestore/properties', () => ({\n126:jest.mock('@/lib/firestore/agent-tasks', () => ({\n130:jest.mock('@/lib/firestore/owner-requests', () => ({\n133:jest.mock(\n137:jest.mock(\n168:function countyRecord(\n222:function proposal(over: Partial<CopilotProposal> = {}): CopilotProposal {\n232:function makeRequest(\n256:function seedExecution(\n278:function stored(uid: string, fp: string) {\n307:describe('confirmed allowlisted proposal → exactly one authorized action', () => {\n308:  it('county_confirm with a single candidate enqueues exactly one confirm task', async () => {\n338:  it('county_refresh on a confirmed parcel enqueues exactly one refresh task', async () => {\n382:  it('task_cancel invokes cancelTask exactly once with the proposal revision', async () => {\n395:  it('task_retry invokes retryTask exactly once with the proposal revision', async () => {\n408:  it('owner_request_draft delegates to the draft endpoint exactly once', async () => {\n432:  it('owner_request_send delegates to the send endpoint exactly once (stable key)', async () => {\n453:describe('rejections invoke no action', () => {\n454:  it('unknown action type → not_allowed, before any read or write', async () => {\n471:  it('proposal-only action (document_approve) → not_allowed, nothing runs', async () => {\n488:  it('tampered payload (fingerprint drift) → tampered, no domain reads at all', async () => {\n502:  it('cross-user county target (not in the caller’s watchlist) → unauthorized', async () => {\n518:  it('cross-user owner request → unauthorized, no delegated call', async () => {\n534:  it('cross-user task (task not found for this owner) → unauthorized', async () => {\n547:  it('stale revision (proposal ≠ current record) → stale, no enqueue', async () => {\n566:  it('a null proposal revision can never CAS → stale', async () => {\n583:  it('duplicate confirmation (the world moved mid-apply) → stale, no second task', async () => {\n607:  it('duplicate task confirmation (revision conflict) → stale, no success', async () => {\n623:  it('task in a terminal/succeeded state → ineligible, nothing runs', async () => {\n641:  it('interrupted send (one already in flight) → interrupted, nothing new', async () => {\n663:  it('non-live county (M1 gate) → ineligible with the honest message', async () => {\n687:describe('durable execution record', () => {\n688:  it('a successful action leaves exactly one terminal record, redacted', async () => {\n718:  it('an audit persistence failure produces NO unrecorded mutation', async () => {\n735:  it('a fresh in-flight record is not re-executed (interrupted)', async () => {\n752:  it('a crashed record (past the grace window) is reclaimed and reconciled', async () => {\n781:  it('a finalize failure after a successful mutation reports audit_unavailable', async () => {\n802:describe('duplicate confirmations replay instead of re-executing', () => {\n803:  it('a second confirmation of an executed action re-executes nothing', async () => {\n829:  it('a second confirmation of a rejected (stale) action replays the rejection', async () => {\n859:describe('redaction, cross-user, malformed proposals', () => {\n860:  it('a delegated-route error carrying an email and a URL is redacted in the record', async () => {\n891:  it('redactSensitive scrubs emails, URLs, and long opaque tokens', () => {\n903:  it('records are owner-scoped: another user starts their own lifecycle', async () => {\n935:  it('a stale revision settles the record as failed with the stale code', async () => {\n959:describe('parseCopilotExecuteRequest', () => {\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788204744713}}
{"type":"message","id":"5c17c342","parentId":"c17c6d56","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:32:30.661Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to look into this further. Let me check the details.\n\nThe test file is 1021 lines, with a comprehensive set of tests. Let's read the fake harness (L45-167) to understand the structure of createFakeDb — that's what I'll need to extend with runTransaction support.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-89c47f0da692a34a","name":"read","arguments":{"limit":130,"offset":40,"path":"/Users/evanhuang/brickspect/dashboard/.worktrees/records-agent-launch/src/lib/__tests__/copilot-actions.test.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":96904,"output":151,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":71,"totalTokens":97055,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788204744716,"responseId":"chatcmpl-b5ab902deda10690","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"3b822355","parentId":"5c17c342","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:32:30.721Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-89c47f0da692a34a","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"  StoredCandidate,\n} from '@/types/county-records';\nimport type { OwnerRequest } from '@/types/owner-requests';\nimport type { WatchlistItem } from '@/types/records-dashboard';\n\n// --- fakes -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ntype StoredRecord = Record<string, unknown>;\n\ninterface FakeOptions {\n  /** Every create() throws (persistence down at claim time). */\n  failCreate?: boolean;\n  /** Updates that move the record to a terminal state throw. */\n  failFinalize?: boolean;\n}\n\n/**\n * In-memory stand-in for the Firestore sub-collection the dispatcher\n * uses (users/{uid}/copilotActionExecutions/{fingerprint}) with the\n * exact semantics the contract relies on: create rejects if the doc\n * exists, update rejects if it does not, get reports exists as a\n * boolean property.\n */\nfunction createFakeDb(over: FakeOptions = {}) {\n  const store = new Map<string, StoredRecord>();\n  const key = (uid: string, fp: string) => `${uid}:${fp}`;\n  const makeDoc = (uid: string, fp: string) => ({\n    get: async () => {\n      const data = store.get(key(uid, fp));\n      return { exists: data !== undefined, data: () => data };\n    },\n    create: async (d: StoredRecord) => {\n      if (over.failCreate) throw new Error('persistence down');\n      const k = key(uid, fp);\n      if (store.has(k)) throw new Error('document already exists');\n      store.set(k, { ...d });\n    },\n    update: async (d: StoredRecord) => {\n      const k = key(uid, fp);\n      const current = store.get(k);\n      if (!current) throw new Error('document not found');\n      if (\n        over.failFinalize &&\n        (d.state === 'succeeded' || d.state === 'failed')\n      ) {\n        throw new Error('persistence down');\n      }\n      store.set(k, { ...current, ...d });\n    },\n    set: async (d: StoredRecord) => {\n      store.set(key(uid, fp), { ...d });\n    },\n  });\n  const db = {\n    collection: (name: string) => {\n      if (name === 'users') {\n        return {\n          doc: (uid: string) => ({\n            collection: (n2: string) => {\n              if (n2 === 'copilotActionExecutions') {\n                return { doc: (fp: string) => makeDoc(uid, fp) };\n              }\n              throw new Error(`unexpected collection ${n2}`);\n            },\n          }),\n        };\n      }\n      throw new Error(`unexpected collection ${name}`);\n    },\n  };\n  return { db, store };\n}\n\nlet mockFakeDb: ReturnType<typeof createFakeDb>['db'];\nlet mockStore: Map<string, StoredRecord>;\n\njest.mock('@/lib/firebase/admin', () => ({\n  getFirestoreDb: () => mockFakeDb,\n}));\njest.mock('@/lib/firestore/county-records', () => ({\n  getCountyRecord: jest.fn(),\n  enqueueCountyTaskIfCurrent: jest.fn(),\n}));\njest.mock('@/lib/firestore/properties', () => ({\n  getWatchlistItem: jest.fn(),\n}));\njest.mock('@/lib/firestore/agent-tasks', () => ({\n  cancelTask: jest.fn(),\n  retryTask: jest.fn(),\n}));\njest.mock('@/lib/firestore/owner-requests', () => ({\n  getOwnerRequest: jest.fn(),\n}));\njest.mock(\n  '@/app/api/properties/[id]/owner-requests/[requestId]/draft/route',\n  () => ({ POST: jest.fn() })\n);\njest.mock(\n  '@/app/api/properties/[id]/owner-requests/[requestId]/send/route',\n  () => ({ POST: jest.fn() })\n);\n\n// --- fixtures ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nconst CUYAHOGA = { code: 'cuyahoga-oh', name: 'Cuyahoga County' };\n\nconst watchlistItem = (over: Partial<WatchlistItem> = {}): WatchlistItem =>\n  ({\n    id: 'prop-1',\n    address: '5180 Broadview Rd',\n    city: 'Cleveland',\n    state: 'OH',\n    zipCode: '44143',\n    county: CUYAHOGA,\n    propertyType: 'residential',\n    title: 'Broadview House',\n    crexiId: 'c1',\n    listingUrl: 'https://crexi.com/x',\n    thumbnailUrl: null,\n    price: 400000,\n    sizeSqFt: 2000,\n    pricePerSqFt: 200,\n    capturedAt: '2026-08-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n    verdict: null,\n    addedAt: '2026-08-01T00:00:00.000Z',\n    ...over,\n  }) as unknown as WatchlistItem;\n\nfunction countyRecord(\n  over: Partial<StoredCountyRecord> = {}\n\n[853 more lines in file. Use offset=170 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788204750721}}
{"type":"message","id":"c5bbe531","parentId":"3b822355","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:33:28.358Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I've got the full picture. This design ripples through the contract, so let me request deeper reasoning for WU3 — transactional reclaim + identifier redaction. Actually, I've already thought through the design pretty well:\n\n1. `claimExecution`: pre-read outside the transaction (the common paths of replay / fresh-inflight don't need a transaction); otherwise `db.runTransaction` — read inside the tx, decide, and do one of the following:\n   - fresh → `tx.create(doc, record)`\n   - replay / interrupted → return without writing (the tx commits empty)\n   - reclaim → `tx.update(doc, { state:'executing', updatedAt, attempts: current.attempts+1 })`\n   \n   Concurrency: two reclaimers both read the stale record; both transactions run; Firestore serializes them — the second commit conflicts (the doc changed after the snapshot read) → the SDK auto-retries (default 6 times) → the re-read sees `executing` + new updatedAt → fresh-inflight → interrupted. Exactly one winner.\n\n   One nuance: the real Firestore admin SDK `runTransaction` retries the closure on abort. If the closure throws, the tx aborts and it retries. If the closure resolves, the commit happens; a commit conflict → retry. So our closure must be pure with respect to the store — and it is.\n\n   About the fake harness: implement `runTransaction(fn)` that executes `fn(tx)` where `tx.get` returns the current store snapshot, `tx.create`/`tx.update` apply against the store, and the fake serializes transactions with a simple async mutex (a per-db queue) so concurrent transactions don't interleave — this models Firestore's serializable behavior. For conflict detection in the fake: capture a read snapshot; if a tx writes to a doc that changed since its snapshot read, throw an abort error once (to simulate the conflict → retry). The simpler and sufficient approach: a mutex makes two \"concurrent\" txs sequential in the fake, and the second one naturally re-reads the new state inside the closure. The real Firestore guarantees this via the isolation mechanism; the fake models it via serialization. The unit tests assert exactly one proceeds; the EMULATOR test (WU3's test list: \"emulator-backed concurrent-reclaim test using a barrier\") provides real concurrent isolation.\n\n2. `recordGateOutcome`: route through the same transactional primitive: read inside the tx; terminal → replay; fresh-inflight → interrupted; stale → update to terminal-failed (CAS within the tx); nonexistent → create as terminal-failed.\n\n3. Identifier transformation: at claim time, store `targetRef: keyedHash(uid, fingerprint, targetId)` and `keyRef: keyedHash(uid, fingerprint, idempotencyKey)`. Keyed hash = sha256 of `${uid}|${fingerprint}|${value}` — irreversibly transformed, stable for correlation. Keep the legacy fields as `targetId?` / `idempotencyKey?` (optional) in the interface for old records; new writes don't include them.\n\n   Wait — is this hash \"sufficient for idempotency\"? The audit says \"store a validated canonical identifier or a keyed hash that is sufficient for traceability/idempotency.\" The idempotency of the DOMAIN action comes from the derived domain key (`copilot:send:...` inside dispatch) and the fingerprint as the doc id — the stored `idempotencyKey` (UI message id) is purely for traceability, not for domain idempotency. So transforming it is safe.\n\n4. `schemaVersion: 2` on new records (shape changed). Backward compatible: `isFreshInFlight`/`isTerminal`/`replayResult` don't touch these fields; `attempts ?? 1`.\n\n5. A note in the header about the crash boundary.\n\n6. The step-3 \"mark as executing\" update in `executeCopilotAction` (a best-effort docRef.update after a fresh claim) — this is also a read-less write, but it's idempotent (state is already executing/accepted) and not a race that would double-dispatch: only the claim winner proceeds. But under the new claim, the winner of a fresh claim is left with state 'accepted' and then step 3 updates it to 'executing' — no problem (it's the sole owner of this record right now). Reclaim has already set it to 'executing' inside the tx. Should step 3 be left as best-effort? Under a concurrent fresh claim, only one winner exists. But if step 3 throws (persistence down), we continue with 'accepted' — the existing behavior is preserved. OK, leave it as is.\n\n   Actually — one subtle point: in step 3 of `executeCopilotAction`, `docRef.update` — if persistence is down, `update` throws, we catch it and continue. Keep as is.\n\nNow, about the test file: I need to update the fake harness with `runTransaction`. Existing tests rely on `create`/`update`/`get` of the fake doc ref — even if the implementation switches claim to tx, if I add runTransaction to the fake and make tx.create/update delegate to the same store semantics, the existing tests will continue to work.\n\nAlso, existing tests that assert on stored records: \"a successful action leaves exactly one terminal record, redacted\" (L688) — probably asserts on fields like targetId/idempotencyKey. Those assertions will have to change to targetRef/keyRef. And the redaction tests (L860, 891), the cross-user one (L903), the stale settle (L935). I need to read and update those assertions.\n\nNew tests to add (unit):\n- concurrent reclaim: two confirmations after the grace period (seed a crashed record), both call executeCopilotAction concurrently (Promise.all) → exactly one `proceed` (domain action mock called once), the other returns 'interrupted'; final record is a single lifecycle (attempts 2, terminal).\n- failure injection: before claim (failCreate → audit_unavailable, no dispatch), after claim / before dispatch (domain action throws → record is terminal-failed with 'failure'), after dispatch / before terminal audit (failFinalize → audit_unavailable).\n- redaction: a targetId containing an email / URL → the record stores only the hash (no verbatim value); the idempotencyKey is transformed.\n- schema: new record has schemaVersion 2; a seeded legacy record (without schemaVersion) is still replayed (backward compat).\n- The rest (replay, stale, cross-user, malformed, audit-unavailable) mostly exist; add the concurrent version.\n\nEmulator test (integration): a concurrent reclaim with a barrier — two actual concurrent POSTs to /api/copilot/proposals/execute after the grace period, asserting exactly one domain effect and one audit lifecycle. That's WU3's \"emulator-backed\" test. I'll put it in `__tests__/integration/firestore/copilot-reclaim.integration.test.ts`. This needs a real route with real Firestore — seeding: a county_confirm proposal? A domain effect = enqueued agent task. Two concurrent confirmations of the same fingerprint after the grace period: one reclaims and dispatches (enqueue a county_confirm task), the other must return interrupted. Emulate grace-period expiry: seed the execution record with updatedAt = 10 minutes ago, state 'executing' (crashed). Then two concurrent POSTs. The winner enqueues the task (agentTasks doc), the loser is interrupted. Assert: agentTasks count = 1, execution record's terminal state with attempts=2, one domain event, etc.\n\nThis is a big chunk of work. Given the project instructions (Subagent Model Routing: \"keep the county reconciliation and Copilot transaction work on a high-reasoning lane\" — from the plan's execution notes L207), and my routing guidance, WU3 should be done by me (the parent) on a high-reasoning lane — the plan explicitly says \"keep the county reconciliation and Copilot transaction work on a high-reasoning lane.\" Since I'm on a qwen3.8-27b local main... wait, the environment section of AGENTS.md says main model is qwen3.8-27b. But I'm operating inside a pi session; the model routing instruction about the \"high-reasoning lane\" means use a strong model — subagents are restricted to local 9B (which is weak for this concurrency work). So I, the parent, should do WU3 myself. OK — continuing in the parent.\n\nSince this is subtle concurrency + contract work, let me request a deeper reasoning pass.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'm going to use `runTransaction` for WU3's transactional reclaim, which is the only approach safe for this kind of concurrency and audit-critical contract work.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a752d5a9ed60ff4b","name":"request_deeper_reasoning","arguments":{"reason":"WU3 rewrites the Copilot dispatcher's claim path from read-then-unconditional-update to a Firestore transaction with exactly-one-winner reclaim semantics (two concurrent post-grace reclaims must serialize so one proceeds and the other gets interrupted/replay), plus identifier redaction (targetId/idempotencyKey → keyed hashes) with backward-compatible schema versioning. Getting the transaction isolation model, retry-on-conflict behavior, and the crash-boundary documentation wrong would silently break the durable-audit guarantee the entire M3 contract rests on."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":98284,"output":2054,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1878,"totalTokens":100338,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788204750724,"responseId":"chatcmpl-91e1a0d26a20382f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
