{"type":"message","id":"5c4279e3","parentId":"e5d2d880","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:33:28.363Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-a752d5a9ed60ff4b","toolName":"request_deeper_reasoning","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deeper reasoning enabled for this task: WU3 rewrites the Copilot dispatcher's claim path from read-then-unconditional-update to a Firestore transaction with exactly-one-winner reclaim semantics (two concurrent post-grace reclaims must serialize so one proceeds and the other gets interrupted/replay), plus identifier redaction (targetId/idempotencyKey → keyed hashes) with backward-compatible schema versioning. Getting the transaction isolation model, retry-on-conflict behavior, and the crash-boundary documentation wrong would silently break the durable-audit guarantee the entire M3 contract rests on."}],"details":{"applied":true,"reason":"WU3 rewrites the Copilot dispatcher's claim path from read-then-unconditional-update to a Firestore transaction with exactly-one-winner reclaim semantics (two concurrent post-grace reclaims must serialize so one proceeds and the other gets interrupted/replay), plus identifier redaction (targetId/idempotencyKey → keyed hashes) with backward-compatible schema versioning. Getting the transaction isolation model, retry-on-conflict behavior, and the crash-boundary documentation wrong would silently break the durable-audit guarantee the entire M3 contract rests on."},"isError":false,"timestamp":1788204808363}}
{"type":"message","id":"78e34766","parentId":"5c4279e3","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:37:38.995Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The deep reasoning is enabled. Before writing the WU3 implementation, let me carefully think through the design:\n\n## WU3 Design\n\n### 1. Transactional claim primitive\n\nA single helper that both `claimExecution` and `recordGateOutcome` use:\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Run a read-decide-write cycle against the execution record under a\n * Firestore transaction, so concurrent claimers serialize: exactly one\n * can transition a crashed record to `executing` (or create a fresh one);\n * the losers re-read inside their (retried) transaction and see the new\n * state — they return replay or interrupted without dispatching.\n */\nasync function withExecutionRecord<T>(\n  uid: string,\n  fingerprint: string,\n  fn: (\n    tx: FirebaseFirestore.Transaction,\n    record: CopilotActionExecution | null\n  ) => T | Promise<T>\n): Promise<{ ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: unknown }> {\n  const db = getFirestoreDb();\n  const docRef = executionDocRef(db, uid, fingerprint);\n  try {\n    const value = await db.runTransaction(async (tx) => {\n      const got = await tx.get(docRef);\n      const record = got.exists ? (got.data() as CopilotActionExecution) : null;\n      return await fn(tx, record);\n    });\n    return { ok: true, value };\n  } catch (error) {\n    return { ok: false, error };\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe important semantics of `db.runTransaction`:\n- The closure runs, and `tx.get` snapshots the doc.\n- Writes in `tx.create` / `tx.update` are staged, and the commit is executed atomically.\n- If the commit conflicts (because a concurrent transaction modified the doc between this snapshot and the commit), the SDK aborts and re-runs the closure (up to `maxAttempts`, default 6). The re-run re-snapshots → sees the winner's new state → the decision flips to replay/interrupted.\n- If the closure itself throws (an intentional abort), the transaction is aborted, retried the same way, and after exhausting attempts the error propagates.\n\nSo my `decide()` + write fits perfectly: it's a pure read-decide-write.\n\nOne important Firestore subtlety: **a transaction that performs no writes at all is committed fine** (an empty commit). The replay/interrupted path returns a result without staging a write — the commit is a no-op. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: `tx.create` throws an \"already exists\" error if the doc was created between the snapshot and the commit — but that failure surfaces as a retryable commit conflict (the SDK aborts + retries; on retry `tx.get` sees the doc → decision flips). In the admin SDK, a `tx.create` on an existing doc throws inside the closure → abort → retry → correct re-decision.\n\n### 2. Rewrite of claimExecution\n\n```ts\nasync function claimExecution(uid, req): Promise<...> {\n  const { proposal, fingerprint, idempotencyKey } = req;\n\n  // Common-path fast read outside the transaction: duplicates and\n  // fresh in-flight confirmations don't need isolation — only\n  // create/reclaim races do.\n  const first = await readExecution(uid, fingerprint);\n  const firstDecision = decide(first);\n  if (firstDecision.kind === 'replay') return { kind: 'replay', result: firstDecision.result };\n  if (firstDecision.kind === 'interrupted') return { kind: 'interrupted', result: firstDecision.result };\n\n  const outcome = await withExecutionRecord(uid, fingerprint, async (tx, record) => {\n    const decision = decide(record);\n    switch (decision.kind) {\n      case 'fresh':\n        tx.create(executionDocRef(getFirestoreDb(), uid, fingerprint), newAcceptedRecord(...));\n        return 'proceed';\n      case 'replay':\n        return `replay:${JSON.stringify(decision.result)}`;  // hmm — awkward\n      ...\n    }\n  });\n}\n```\n\nReturning a rich union from the closure works fine — it's just a value. There's no serialization constraint (the closure's return value is returned by runTransaction). Let's return the union directly:\n\n```ts\nconst decision = await withExecutionRecord(uid, fingerprint, (tx, record) => {\n  const d = decide(record);\n  if (d.kind === 'fresh') {\n    tx.create(docRef, {...accepted record, schemaVersion: 2, targetRef, keyRef, attempts: 1});\n    return { kind: 'proceed' as const };\n  }\n  if (d.kind === 'replay') return { kind: 'replay', result: d.result } as const;\n  if (d.kind === 'interrupted') return { kind: 'interrupted', result: d.result } as const;\n  // reclaim\n  tx.update(docRef, { state: 'executing', updatedAt: nowIso(), attempts: (d.record.attempts ?? 1) + 1 });\n  return { kind: 'proceed' as const };\n});\n```\n\nIf the withExecutionRecord catch fires (persistence down / retries exhausted) → `{ kind: 'unavailable' }`.\n\nWait — one problem: the current `decide()` treats \"stale non-terminal\" as reclaimable. The old code's reclaim path did `docRef.update` unconditionally — that was the race. The new path stages the update inside the tx: if the doc was modified after the tx snapshot, the commit conflicts → retry → re-decide. Exactly one winner.\n\nBut there's a subtlety about what \"reclaim\" writes: it updates `state: 'executing'` and `updatedAt`. Two concurrent reclaimers:\n- T1 snapshots R (crashed: state 'executing', updatedAt T0, attempts N).\n- T2 snapshots the same R.\n- T1 stages the update (executing, updatedAt now1, attempts N+1) and commits → doc is now {executing, now1, N+1}.\n- T2's commit conflicts (doc changed after its snapshot) → abort → re-run closure → new snapshot → sees {executing, now1, N+1} → now1 is fresh (well within the grace period) → isFreshInFlight → decision is 'interrupted' → no write → T2 returns interrupted.\n\nExactly one dispatch.\n\nAnd for a fresh-create race: T1/T2 both snapshot \"no doc\" → both stage tx.create → the first commit wins; the second create fails (doc exists) → abort → retry → snapshot sees the new 'accepted' record (updatedAt = now, fresh) → interrupted. Exactly one.\n\nNote on the fake harness: it must serialize transactions (a per-db mutex queue) and re-snapshot on retry. The simplest, most faithful fake: runTransaction(fn) = push onto a serialized queue; each run: call fn(tx) once, where tx.get returns a snapshot of the current store at fn call time, and tx.create/update mutate the store at commit time (after fn resolves). To emulate a conflict: after fn resolves, compare the state of the docs read within the closure against the store at commit time — if a doc that was read was modified after the snapshot (impossible within a single serialized run, since the queue is exclusive)… actually, under strict serialization, conflicts never occur in the fake: the second run just sees the new state directly on snapshot. That's faithful enough — Firestore's retry is how it detects that state; serialization achieves the same outcome (the second re-reads the new state). The EMULATOR test covers real concurrency.\n\nHmm, but there's one nuance: in real Firestore, if the commit conflicts, the closure is re-run. In the fake under serialization, the closure only runs once per runTransaction call, but the snapshot is fresh at call time. The outcome (exactly one winner) is the same.\n\n### 3. Rewrite of recordGateOutcome\n\n```ts\nasync function recordGateOutcome(uid, req, code, message): Promise<CopilotExecuteResult> {\n  const result = { code, message };\n  const outcome = await withExecutionRecord(uid, fingerprint, (tx, record) => {\n    if (record && isTerminal(record)) return { kind: 'replay', result: replayResult(record) };\n    if (record && isFreshInFlight(record)) return { kind: 'interrupted', result: {...} };\n    // create or settle as terminal-failed\n    const docRef = ...;\n    if (record) {\n      tx.update(docRef, { state: 'failed', code, message: redactSensitive(message), action: null, updatedAt: nowIso() });\n    } else {\n      tx.create(docRef, {...terminal failed record, schemaVersion: 2, targetRef, keyRef, attempts: 1, acceptedAt: t, updatedAt: t});\n    }\n    return { kind: 'settled' as const };\n  });\n  // map: replay → return replay result; interrupted → that; settled → return result; unavailable → audit_unavailable\n}\n```\n\n### 4. Identifier transformation\n\n```ts\n/**\n * A keyed, irreversibly transformed reference to caller-supplied\n * identifier material: a sha256 keyed by (owner, fingerprint, field),\n * so the audit record remains correlatable (the same input always\n * maps to the same ref for the same owner+proposal) but can never\n * be used to recover the raw value — a hostile or model-generated\n * input can no longer smuggle PII/secrets into the durable audit.\n */\nexport function keyedIdentifierRef(uid: string, fingerprint: string, field: 'target' | 'key', value: string): string {\n  return sha256Hex(`${uid}|${fingerprint}|${field}|${value}`);\n}\n```\n\nRecord fields: new writes store `targetRef` and `keyRef`; the `targetId`/`idempotencyKey` fields disappear from new writes. The interface: make those two optional (legacy v1 records may have them), add `schemaVersion?: number`… no, the plan says \"version the execution schema if needed and stay backward-compatible.\" So:\n\n```ts\nexport const EXECUTION_SCHEMA_VERSION = 2;\n\nexport interface CopilotActionExecution {\n  /** 2 = identifiers are stored as keyed refs (v1 records may have raw targetId/idempotencyKey). */\n  schemaVersion?: number;\n  /** v2: keyed hash of the caller's targetId (the raw value is no longer persisted). */\n  targetRef?: string;\n  /** v2: keyed hash of the caller's idempotencyKey. */\n  keyRef?: string;\n  /** v1 (legacy): the raw caller-controlled values — read for replay\n   *  compatibility only, never written by current code. */\n  targetId?: string;\n  idempotencyKey?: string;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nAnd replayResult only uses code/message/action — no change. isFreshInFlight/isTerminal — no change.\n\nWhere else is `targetId` read from the record? Search the codebase for usages of CopilotActionExecution — probably only this module (the record is internal to the dispatcher; there may be a UI that reads the execution record? Let me grep for 'copilotActionExecutions'). If the UI is reading it, the field removal could break it — but it's unlikely (owner-scoped audit collection; the plan says redact). Let me verify.\n\n### 5. Documentation of the crash boundary\n\nAdd to the module header (or the claimExecution docstring):\n\n```\n * Crash boundary (documented, by design non-atomic): the domain\n * mutation (a county enqueue / task CAS / mail send) and the terminal\n * audit transition live in different collections — and the owner-request\n * seams in other modules entirely — so they cannot be one Firestore\n * transaction. A crash after the mutation but before the terminal write\n * leaves a non-terminal record; the next confirmation of the same\n * fingerprint reclaims it and re-dispatches through the same\n * idempotent/CAS-keyed seam, which makes the second attempt a\n * deterministic no-op (duplicate task enqueue → stale; duplicate\n * cancel/retry → revision conflict; duplicate send → the mail\n * idempotency key). The audit therefore always catches up; the domain\n * never double-applies.\n```\n\n### 6. The step-3 executing-mark in executeCopilotAction\n\nKeep the best-effort update (it's just a cosmetic state advance for a fresh claim; the claim winner is the sole writer until finalize). Actually — under the new design, should the fresh-claim path set 'executing' directly inside the claim transaction? No: claim = 'accepted' is a contract (\"the record is claimed before the domain action runs\"). The later 'executing' mark is a separate best-effort step. Leave it as is, though it could also be made transaction-safe… this is a single-owner update (only the claim winner reaches it), so a plain update is fine — the race that needed fixing was the claim/reclaim race. Leave it as is.\n\nAlso: the step-3 `docRef.update` — no change to the fake harness.\n\n### Test changes\n\n1. Add `runTransaction` to the fake DB: a per-db serialization queue.\n\n```ts\nfunction createFakeDb(over: FakeOptions = {}) {\n  const store = new Map<string, StoredRecord>();\n  const key = ...;\n  let txQueue: Promise<unknown> = Promise.resolve();\n  const runSerialized = <T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): { promise: Promise<T>, enqueue: boolean } => {...}\n  \n  const db = {\n    collection: ... (same, makeDoc now includes get/create/update/set for the doc),\n    runTransaction: async (fn) => {\n      // Serialize concurrent transactions the way Firestore's\n      // snapshot isolation serializes conflicting writers: each run\n      // snapshots the store at its turn and sees everything earlier\n      // committed.\n      const run = async () => {\n        const tx = {\n          get: async (docRef) => { snapshot the doc at call time },\n          create: async (docRef, d) => { stage },\n          update: async (docRef, d) => { stage },\n          set: async (docRef, d, opts) => { stage },\n        };\n        // The admin SDK's runTransaction: call fn; if the closure\n        // throws, retry up to N times; commit on success.\n        for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 6; attempt++) {\n          try {\n            const staged = new Map();\n            const value = await fn(makeTx());\n            // commit: apply the staged writes\n            ...\n            return value;\n          } catch (error) {\n            if (attempt === 5) throw error;\n            // abort → retry (re-snapshot)\n          }\n        }\n      };\n      const p = txQueue.then(run);\n      txQueue = p.catch(() => {});\n      return p;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThe shape of docRef matters: the closure receives the docRef object and calls `tx.get(docRef)` — the fake docRef must be identifiable. The fake makeDoc returns a plain object; tx.get needs to know the (uid, fp) of the doc. Give each fake doc a hidden symbol, or make the doc object carry a `__key`. Let's restructure: `makeDoc(uid, fp)` returns an object with get/create/update/set AND a `__key: key(uid, fp)`. Then tx.get(doc) → uses doc.__key.\n\nAlso, `executionDocRef` is called with `getFirestoreDb()` inside withExecutionRecord — no problem.\n\nFailure injection for the fake: `failTransaction?: boolean` — the first commit of a tx containing a write throws 'persistence down' → the runTransaction retry loop: after the maxAttempts retries, it propagates → withExecutionRecord catches → unavailable. But careful: a retry that re-runs the closure with the same failing commit → it must fail after a few retries to keep the tests fast (the real SDK defaults to 6; use 3 for the fake, and failFinalize-style injection only on the first attempt? The simplest: `failTransactionAttempts?: number` — fail the commit of the first N attempts).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: do I really need retry semantics in the fake? The only way the unit tests exercise the \"reclaim race\" is via serialized runs: two runTransaction calls enqueued in parallel → the first commits the claim → the second snapshots the new state → interrupted. No retry needed for that. Failure injection (audit_unavailable) needs: a commit failure → the retry loop → eventually throw → catch → unavailable. If the fake fails only the first commit and succeeds on retry, the tx would \"succeed\" on retry — that's not what failCreate currently tests (\"an audit-persistence failure produces no unrecorded mutation\" — the claim fails entirely). With the real SDK, if Firestore is down, every attempt fails → propagate. So the fake: `failTransaction?: boolean` → every commit attempt throws → the runTransaction loop exhausts (3 tries) → throw → unavailable. That matches.\n\nThe existing test \"an audit-persistence failure produces no unrecorded mutation\" uses failCreate — it currently throws on docRef.create. In the new implementation, a fresh claim uses tx.create inside the tx → the fake's tx commit fails when `over.failTransaction`… I should add a failTransaction option and migrate that test to use it (or, to keep the test working unchanged, make tx.commit also honor failCreate when the staged ops include a create). Cleaner: honor `failCreate` at commit time if any staged op is a create, and `failFinalize` if any staged op sets a terminal state. That way, the existing failure-injection tests work without change! And the update-only path (step-3 executing mark) uses docRef.update directly (outside the tx) — failFinalize only fires on terminal states, so that's unaffected.\n\nWait, step 3 calls `docRef.update({state: 'executing'...})` — under failFinalize, the update only throws on terminal states; 'executing' passes through. Good, matches current behavior.\n\nFinalize (step 5) also uses a raw `docRef.update` to terminal — failFinalize throws → audit_unavailable. Existing test 781. Good.\n\n2. Existing record-shape assertions: \"a successful action leaves exactly one terminal record, redacted\" (L688) — I need to read it to see which fields it asserts. It probably asserts targetId/idempotencyKey — update to targetRef/keyRef + schemaVersion.\n\n3. New unit tests:\n   - **Concurrent reclaim (fake)**: seed a crashed record (state 'executing', updatedAt = now - 10 min, attempts 1). Two Promise.all executeCopilotAction calls with the same request → exactly one 'executed', the other 'interrupted'; the domain seam is called once; the record's attempts === 2, terminal.\n     - Wait: the winner reclaims (attempts → 2) and dispatches; the loser is interrupted. The final record's attempts = 2 (the loser doesn't write).\n   - **Concurrent fresh claim**: no record; two concurrent confirmations → one proceeds (creates, then executes), the other is interrupted.\n     - Note: in the fake, run 1 creates the record inside the tx; run 2 snapshots it (accepted, updatedAt now → fresh in-flight) → interrupted.\n   - **Redaction of identifiers**: a proposal targetId containing an email/URL (e.g., 'victim@example.com') + an idempotencyKey containing a URL → the record stores targetRef/keyRef (64-char hex), and does not contain the raw values anywhere in the record (JSON.stringify(record) must not contain the email).\n   - **schemaVersion + backward compat**: a new record has schemaVersion 2; a seeded v1 record (with raw targetId, no schemaVersion, terminal) → the duplicate replays correctly.\n   - **Gate outcome under a crashed record**: a tampered confirmation against a crashed (stale) record → settles the record as failed/tampered, exactly once (two concurrent tampered confirmations → one settles, the other interrupted… hmm: both see stale; T1 settles to terminal-failed; T2's commit conflicts → retry → sees terminal → replays the T1 result (tampered). So the second gets a replay of the tampered result — that's fine (same code). Assert: the record is settled once (attempts stays 1? A settle via update does not increment attempts — the current code also doesn't increment on a gate settle; preserve that), both callers get 'tampered'.\n\n4. Emulator integration test (concurrent reclaim with a barrier):\n   - Seed: a watchlist item (Cuyahoga, live coverage), a county record in confirmation_required with one candidate (revision N) — via saveResolution (real seam).\n   - Seed a crashed execution record directly into copilotActionExecutions (raw write — the test can use the admin SDK directly; the record must be state 'executing', updatedAt = now - 10 min, attempts 1, with a matching fingerprint for the proposal).\n     - Hmm — seeding the execution record requires the fingerprint = proposalFingerprint(proposal). Computable in the test (import proposalFingerprint or sha256Hex + canonicalProposalString).\n   - Two concurrent POSTs to /api/copilot/proposals/execute with the same body.\n   - Assert: exactly one 'executed' response, one 'interrupted'; agentTasks subcollection = 1 doc (the county_confirm task); the execution record is terminal-succeeded, attempts 2, action 'county_confirm'; domainEvents +1.\n   - Wait — but a concurrent county_confirm enqueue: the winner reclaims the audit record and dispatches county_confirm → enqueueCountyTaskIfCurrent with record.revision as expectedRevision → the CAS in the enqueue… the enqueue checks record revision CAS inside its own transaction; a single enqueue succeeds. The loser doesn't dispatch at all (interrupted at the claim stage) → no second enqueue.\n   - Barrier: two concurrent fetches — the true concurrency is provided by real parallel HTTP against the real Firestore.\n\nLet me also verify the execute route (src/app/api/copilot/proposals/execute/route.ts) — I read it in an earlier session; it calls executeCopilotAction with the parsed body. Fine.\n\nOne more check: is anything else reading `copilotActionExecutions` (a UI or report route)? Let me grep. And check the usages of the CopilotActionExecution type elsewhere.\n\nNow let me grep for these, then write the implementation.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nI'll verify that nothing else reads the execution record shape or collection, then implement.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b60e6a633d17cca7","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -rn \"copilotActionExecutions\\|CopilotActionExecution\" src/ --include=\"*.ts\" --include=\"*.tsx\" | grep -v \"copilot-actions.ts\\|copilot-actions.test.ts\" | head; echo ---; sed -n '688,735p' src/lib/__tests__/copilot-actions.test.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen38-main","model":"qwen3.8-27b","usage":{"input":100508,"output":5132,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":5011,"totalTokens":105640,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788204808368,"responseId":"chatcmpl-83bca67bd40f3b81","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"message","id":"a191c56d","parentId":"78e34766","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:37:39.106Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b60e6a633d17cca7","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/app/api/copilot/proposals/execute/route.ts:39: * execution record (copilotActionExecutions) keyed by the proposal\n---\n  it('a successful action leaves exactly one terminal record, redacted', async () => {\n    (getWatchlistItem as jest.Mock).mockResolvedValue(watchlistItem());\n    (getCountyRecord as jest.Mock).mockResolvedValue(countyRecord());\n    const req = makeRequest(\n      proposal({\n        actionType: 'county_confirm',\n        targetId: 'prop-1',\n        revision: 7,\n      })\n    );\n\n    const res = await executeCopilotAction('u1', req);\n\n    expect(res.code).toBe('executed');\n    expect(mockStore.size).toBe(1);\n    const record = stored('u1', req.fingerprint);\n    expect(record).toBeDefined();\n    expect(record?.state).toBe('succeeded');\n    expect(record?.code).toBe('executed');\n    expect(record?.action).toBe('county_confirm');\n    expect(record?.fingerprint).toMatch(/^[a-f0-9]{64}$/);\n    expect(record?.idempotencyKey).toBe('ui:message-1');\n    expect(record?.attempts).toBe(1);\n    // Redacted by construction: no addresses, no emails, no URLs.\n    const serialized = JSON.stringify(record);\n    expect(serialized).not.toContain('Broadview');\n    expect(serialized).not.toContain('@');\n    expect(serialized).not.toContain('http');\n  });\n\n  it('an audit persistence failure produces NO unrecorded mutation', async () => {\n    ({ db: mockFakeDb, store: mockStore } = createFakeDb({ failCreate: true }));\n\n    const res = await executeCopilotAction(\n      'u1',\n      makeRequest(\n        proposal({ actionType: 'task_retry', targetId: 'task-1', revision: 2 })\n      )\n    );\n\n    expect(res.code).toBe('audit_unavailable');\n    // The claim failed before any domain read or write.\n    expect(retryTask).not.toHaveBeenCalled();\n    expect(cancelTask).not.toHaveBeenCalled();\n    expect(mockStore.size).toBe(0);\n  });\n\n  it('a fresh in-flight record is not re-executed (interrupted)', async () => {\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788205059106}}
