unknown> {\n  const t = nowIso();\n  return {\n    schemaVersion: EXECUTION_SCHEMA_VERSION,\n    actionType: req.proposal.actionType,\n    targetRef: keyedIdentifierRef(uid, req.fingerprint, 'target', req.proposal.targetId),\n    keyRef: keyedIdentifierRef(uid, req.fingerprint, 'key', req.idempotencyKey),\n    fingerprint: req.fingerprint,\n    ...fields,\n    acceptedAt: t,\n    updatedAt: t,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait — but `acceptedAt` on UPDATE (reclaim) must not be reset. Only on create. So two builders, or a parameter. Let me have the create path use buildExecutionRecord (with acceptedAt) and the update path use a plain field object.\n\n6. `withExecutionRecord` primitive:\n\n```ts\n/**\n * Run a read-decide-write cycle against the execution record under a\n * Firestore transaction...\n */\nasync function withExecutionRecord<T>(\n  uid: string,\n  fingerprint: string,\n  fn: (\n    tx: ReturnType<typeof getFirestoreDb>['runTransaction'] extends (\n      f: (tx: infer Tx) => unknown,\n      ...\n    ) => unknown ? Tx : never,\n    record: CopilotActionExecution | null\n  ) => T | Promise<T>\n): Promise<{ ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: unknown }> {\n```\n\nTyping the tx parameter: `import type { Transaction } from '@google-cloud/firestore'`? Let me check which firestore admin package is used — the `getFirestoreDb` in `@/lib/firebase/admin`. Let me check what it returns (probably getFirestore() from @google-cloud/firestore). The Transaction type is importable from '@google-cloud/firestore'. Simpler: define fn as `(tx: any, record) => ...` — no, let me avoid any. Use `import type { Transaction } from '@google-cloud/firestore'`. Let me check firebase/admin.ts.\n\nActually, the cleanest is:\n\n```ts\ntype FirestoreDb = ReturnType<typeof getFirestoreDb>;\n```\n\nAnd the tx type via a conditional type is ugly. Let me just import the Transaction type. Check the package: `src/lib/firebase/admin.ts` — probably `import { getFirestore } from '@google-cloud/firestore'