  * identifier material. The hash is keyed by (owner, proposal\n+ 245  * fingerprint, field), so the same input always maps to the same\n+ 246  * reference for the same owner and proposal — the record stays\n+ 247  * correlatable for traceability and idempotency — while the raw value\n+ 248  * cannot be recovered from the audit store. A hostile or\n+ 249  * model-generated input can no longer smuggle PII or secrets into a\n+ 250  * durable record.\n+ 251  */\n+ 252 export function keyedIdentifierRef(\n+ 253   uid: string,\n+ 254   fingerprint: string,\n+ 255   field: 'target' | 'key',\n+ 256   value: string\n+ 257 ): string {\n+ 258   return sha256Hex(`${uid}|${fingerprint}|${field}|${value}`);\n+ 259 }\n+ 260 \n+ 261 /**\n+ 262  * Build the durable record as CURRENT code writes it (schema v2):\n+ 263  * caller-controlled identifiers persist only as keyed refs (see\n+ 264  * keyedIdentifierRef) — never verbatim.\n+ 265  */\n+ 266 function buildExecutionRecord(\n+ 267   uid: string,\n+ 268   req: CopilotExecuteRequest,\n+ 269   fields: {\n+ 270     state: CopilotActionState;\n+ 271     code: CopilotActionCode | null;\n+ 272     message: string;\n+ 273     action: string | null;\n+ 274     attempts: number;\n+ 275   }\n+ 276 ) {\n+ 277   const t = nowIso();\n+ 278   return {\n+ 279     schemaVersion: EXECUTION_SCHEMA_VERSION,\n+ 280     actionType: req.proposal.actionType,\n+ 281     targetRef: keyedIdentifierRef(\n+ 282       uid,\n+ 283       req.fingerprint,\n+ 284       'target',\n+ 285       req.proposal.targetId\n+ 286     ),\n+ 287     keyRef: keyedIdentifierRef(uid, req.fingerprint, 'key', req.idempotencyKey),\n+ 288     fingerprint: req.fingerprint,\n+ 289     ...fields,\n+ 290     acceptedAt: t,\n+ 291     updatedAt: t,\n+ 292   };\n+ 293 }\n+ 294 \n+ 295 /**\n+ 296  * Run a read-decide-write cycle against the execution record under a\n+ 297  * Firestore transaction. The snapshot read inside the transaction is\n+ 298  * what serializes concurrent writers: if