{"type":"message","id":"d2adc6ed","parentId":"36ab8ddf","timestamp":"2026-08-31T19:49:55.725Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to investigate this further. Let me check the details.\n\nThe staged variable is declared as `let stagedX: T | null = null` after it's used in the tx closure — TypeScript narrows a variable that is only assigned inside a closure to `null` (never) at the point of use. This is a classic problem: TS's control flow analysis doesn't track assignments that happen inside a closure that is called before the declaration site in source order… The actual problem: I declared `let stagedCreate: ... | null = null;` after the definition of `tx` (I wrote the declaration after `const tx = {...}`). No wait — looking at the edit: I wrote the `stagedCreate` assignment inside tx.create before the `let stagedCreate` declaration that appears below. In source order, the `let` declaration is after the `tx` object literal. Since `let` is hoisted within the block, at runtime it's fine (the closure runs later), but TS's flow analysis: at the point of `if (stagedCreate)` inside runOnce, TS thinks the variable's type is narrowed to… The problem: because the initial value is `null` and TS's flow analysis doesn't consider the closure's assignment as occurring before the `if` check (since it's a closure), it narrows `stagedCreate` to `null` at the point of the `if` → `stagedCreate.doc` errors out as type 'never'.\n\nThe fix: declare the staged variable with a type that doesn't get narrowed to null before use — for example, declare it before `tx`, and either use a single object `const staged = { create: null as X | null, update: ..., set: ... }`, or avoid the null narrowing: use `let stagedCreate: X | null = null`, but that's exactly the problem — does TS narrow to `null` after the `fn(tx)` await? Actually, the classic fix: TS resets the narrowing after an `await`… no, that's not it. A reliable pattern:\n\n```ts\nconst staged = {\n  create: null as { doc: { __key: string }; d: StoredRecord } | null,\n  update: null as ... ,\n  set: null as ... ,\n};\nconst tx = {\n  create: async (doc, d) => { staged.create = { doc, d }; },\n  ...\n};\nconst value = await fn(tx);\nif (staged.create) { ... use staged.create ... }\n```\n\nA property on an object doesn't get narrowed to null the same way (property access isn't aggressively narrowed by CFA in this pattern — well, actually it can be narrowed, but since the assignment happens inside a closure, the property retains its declared type). This should work.\n\nAlso, I'll fix the weird ordering: move the `staged` declaration to before `tx`. Let me rewrite that section.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nClassic TS closure-narrowing: the `let ... = null` declared after the closure gets narrowed to `never` at the check. Fixing with a single `staged` object whose properties keep their declared type:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad76465d26706788","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"      const runOnce = async () => {\n        // Staged writes — properties (not standalone lets) so the\n        // closure assignments are visible to flow analysis at commit.\n        const staged = {\n          create: null as\n            | { doc: { __key: string }; d: StoredRecord }\n            | null,\n          update: null as\n            | { doc: { __key: string }; d: StoredRecord }\n            | null,\n          set: null as { doc: { __key: string }; d: StoredRecord } | null,\n        };\n        // Snapshot: tx.get reads the CURRENT store state at this run's\n        // turn (each run sees everything the earlier runs committed).\n        const tx = {\n          get: async (doc: { __key: string }) => {\n            const data = store.get(doc.__key);\n            return { exists: data !== undefined, data: () => data };\n          },\n          create: async (doc: { __key: string }, d: StoredRecord) => {\n            if (over.failCreate) throw new Error('persistence down');\n            // Staged — applied at commit, where the exists-check and the\n            // failure injection both live (commit-time semantics).\n            staged.create = { doc, d };\n          },\n          update: async (doc: { __key: string }, d: StoredRecord) => {\n            staged.update = { doc, d };\n          },\n          set: async (doc: { __key: string }, d: StoredRecord) => {\n            staged.set = { doc, d };\n          },\n        };\n        const value = await fn(tx);\n        // Commit: apply the staged writes atomically (failures here\n        // abort the run, exactly like a failed Firestore commit).\n        if (staged.create) {\n          if (over.failCreate) throw new Error('persistence down');\n          if (store.has(staged.create.doc.__key))\n            throw new Error('document already exists');\n          store.set(staged.create.doc.__key, { ...staged.create.d });\n        }\n        if (staged.update) {\n          const current = store.get(staged.update.doc.__key);\n          if (!current) throw new Error('document not found');\n          if (\n            over.failFinalize &&\n            (staged.update.d.state === 'succeeded' ||\n              staged.update.d.state === 'failed')\n          ) {\n            throw new Error('persistence down');\n          }\n          store.set(staged.update.doc.__key, {\n            ...current,\n            ...staged.update.d,\n          });\n        }\n        if (staged.set) {\n          store.set(staged.set.doc.__key, { ...staged.set.d });\n        }\n        return value;\n      };","oldText":"      const runOnce = async () => {\n        // Snapshot: tx.get reads the CURRENT store state at this run's\n        // turn (each run sees everything the earlier runs committed).\n        const tx = {\n          get: async (doc: { __key: string }) => {\n            const data = store.get(doc.__key);\n            return { exists: data !== undefined, data: () => data };\n          },\n          create: async (doc: { __key: string }, d: StoredRecord) => {\n            if (over.failCreate) throw new Error('persistence down');\n            // Staged — applied at commit, where the exists-check and the\n            // failure injection both live (commit-time semantics).\n            stagedCreate = { doc, d };\n          },\n          update: async (doc: { __key: string }, d: StoredRecord) => {\n            stagedUpdate = { doc, d };\n          },\n          set: async (doc: { __key: string }, d: StoredRecord) => {\n            stagedSet = { doc, d };\n          },\n        };\n        let stagedCreate: {\n          doc: { __key: string };\n          d: StoredRecord;\n        } | null = null;\n        let stagedUpdate: {\n          doc: { __key: string };\n          d: StoredRecord;\n        } | null = null;\n        let stagedSet: {\n          doc: { __key: string };\n          d: StoredRecord;\n        } | null = null;\n        const value = await fn(tx);\n        // Commit: apply the staged writes atomically (failures here\n        // abort the run, exactly like a failed Firestore commit).\n        if (stagedCreate) {\n          if (over.failCreate) throw new Error('persistence down');\n          if (store.has(stagedCreate.doc.__key))\n            throw new Error('document already exists');\n          store.set(stagedCreate.doc.__key, { ...stagedCreate.d });\n        }\n        if (stagedUpdate) {\n          const current = store.get(stagedUpdate.doc.__key);\n          if (!current) throw new Error('document not found');\n          if (\n            over.failFinalize &&\n            (stagedUpdate.d.state === 'succeeded' ||\n              stagedUpdate.d.state === 'failed')\n          ) {\n            throw new Error('persistence down');\n          }\n          store.set(stagedUpdate.doc.__key, {\n            ...current,\n            ...stagedUpdate.d,\n          });\n        }\n        if (stagedSet) {\n    